VERSION:2.0
PRODID:ics.py - http://git.io/lLljaA
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Guitar icon and composer Bill Frisell plays a solo set dedicated to his decades-long connection to Roulette.\n“Since the early days on West Broadway Roulette has been and continues to be an oasis for me. A place for adventure\, inspiration\, imagination. A safe place to take chances. A place to connect. I’ve been given so many opportunities there. I’ve heard so much music there. Things that could have only happened there. Seeds were planted long ago that are still growing today while new ones are being planted every day. I’m very thankful.”– Bill Frisell\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nRoulette member benefits do not apply.\nBill Frisell’s\ncareer as a guitarist and composer has spanned more than 40 years and many celebrated recordings\, whose catalog has been cited by Downbeat as “the best recorded output of the decade.”\nIn recent years\, Frisell has forged a distinctive and fruitful collaboration with the Blue Note label\, releasing\nHARMONY\n\,\nValentine\n\,\nFOUR\n\,\nOrchestras\nand\nIn My Dreams\nto great acclaim.\n“Frisell has had a lot of practice putting high concept into a humble package. Long hailed as one of the most distinctive and original improvising guitarists of our time\, he has also earned a reputation for teasing out thematic connections with his music… There’s a reason that Jazz at Lincoln Center had him program a series called Roots of Americana.”  – New York Times\nRecognized as one of America’s 21 most vital and productive performing artists\, Frisell was named an inaugural Doris Duke Artist in 2012.  He is also a recipient of grants from United States Artists\, Meet the Composer among others.  In 2016\, he was a beneficiary of the first FreshGrass Composition commission to preserve and support innovative grassroots music.  Upon San Francisco Jazz opening their doors in 2013\, he served as one of their Resident Artistic Directors.  Bill is the subject of a documentary film by director Emma Franz\, entitled Bill Frisell: A Portrait\, which examines his creative process in depth\, as well as an extensive biography by Philip Watson\, Beautiful Dreamer: The Guitarist Who Changed The Sound of American Music.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261213T010000Z
SUMMARY:Bill Frisell Solo: A Concert to Benefit Roulette
UID:63fab505-d00c-4161-aa18-9361a022bef7@63fa.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/a-concert-to-benefit-roulette-bill-frisell/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nTwo evenings bring together artists representing the radical spirit that continues to shape the landscape of contemporary music.\n\nA two-night mini-festival celebrating the 80th anniversary of the Internationales Musikinstitut Darmstadt’s groundbreaking Summer Course for New Music—the global meeting point for postwar experimentation\, exchange\, and creative risk.\nNight two features the New York premiere of legendary composer and NEA Jazz Master\nAnthony Braxton’s\nmost recent concert work\,\nThunder Music\n\, which debuted at Darmstadt in 2023\, performed by a group including members of\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble\n.\nGBSR Duo\nopen the evening with a performance of\nKarlheinz Stockhausen’s\nKontakte\n(1958-60).\nThe festival will also include archival listening sessions and lectures exploring Darmstadt’s historic legacy and its living\, evolving influence on today’s artistic practice. Additional details to be announced.\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble x Tri-Centric plays Anthony Braxton’s Thunder Music\nCarl Testa\ndouble bass and electronics\nLevy Lorenzo\npercussion\nIngrid Laubrock\nsaxophone\nDarius Jones\nsaxophone\nReut Regev\ntrombone\nChris McIntyre\ntrombone\nRebekah Heller\nbassoon and conductor\nJonathan Finlayson\ntrumpet\nNick Hallett\nvoice\nKyoko Kitamura\nvoice\nRaquel Klein\nvoice\nAdam Matlock\nvoice and accordion\nAnne Rhodes\nvoice\nFay Victor\nvoice\nGBSR Duo plays Stockhausen’s Kontakte\nGeorge Barton\npercussion\nSiwan Rhys\npiano\n“While not strictly dramatic in nature\,\nThunder Music\nsuggested a stagelike feel. In this new category of (Braxton’s) compositional practice\, individual musicians are responsible for making choices about how to merge their own sound with prerecorded sounds of thunder and nature.”—\nNew York Times in 2023\nOrganized by\nNick Hallett\nand\nZach Layton\n\, curators of NYC’s Darmstadt: Classics of the Avant-Garde\, together with\nThomas Schäfer\n\, director of the IMD\, and\nGeorge Lewis\n\, Artistic Director of the International Contemporary Ensemble\, the festival bridges generations of experimental sound. Envisioned in partnership with a consortium including\nRoulette Intermedium\, The New School\, Fridman Gallery\,\nand the\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble\nis a leading contemporary music based in New York\, currently directed by George Lewis. Founded in 2001 and led by instrumentalists from Ohio’s Oberlin Conservatory including Claire Chase\, Joshua Rubin\, Ross Karre\, and Rebekah Heller\, the ensemble features a flexible collective of instrumentalists and vocalists performing repertoire ranging from solo works to large ensemble pieces. Praised by\nThe New York Times\nas “one of the most accomplished and adventurous groups in new music\,” the Ensemble has premiered more than 500 compositions\, many by emerging composers\, in venues worldwide. The ensemble is recognized for innovative programming\, interdisciplinary collaboration\, and educational initiatives that connect new music with diverse audiences. The Ensemble has received numerous honors\, including the American Music Center Trailblazer Award and Musical America’s 2013 Ensemble of the Year award. The Ensemble has collaborated with conductors including John Adams and Susanna Mälkki and performs internationally at major festivals and concert halls.\nGBSR Duo\nPercussionist George Barton and pianist Siwan Rhys are one of the UK’s leading contemporary chamber ensembles\, celebrated for fearless performances\, ambitious commissions\, and inventive collaborations. Their repertoire spans twentieth-century modernism\, experimental music\, and cross-genre projects\, with programs featuring composers from Karlheinz Stockhausen and Galina Ustvolskaya to Brian Eno and Aphex Twin. The duo has premiered works by Eva-Maria Houben\, Cassie Kinoshi\, Linda Catlin Smith\, and Oliver Leith. They are winners of the Royal Philharmonic Society 2025 Young Artist Award. GBSR has performed internationally with ensembles and artists including the Los Angeles Philharmonic\, Basel Sinfonietta\, and Thomas Adès. In 2026\, they serve as Artists in Residence at Kings Place.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261212T010000Z
SUMMARY:Darmstadt at 80 Festival: International Contemporary Ensemble x Tri-Centric plays Anthony Braxton’s Thunder Music // GBSR Duo plays Stockhausen’s Kontakte
UID:19c565ed-bd0e-4c93-80c3-97cd3767d2d7@19c5.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/darmstadt-at-80-international-contemporary-ensemble-x-tri-centric-anthony-braxtons-thunder-music/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Triple Canopy’s annual symposium is a manifestation of the magazine’s concerns and collaborations\, in person and in real time. The multiday gathering brings together artists\, writers\, performers\, and researchers to address the role of culture in responding (or contributing) to a range of critical issues.\nFree w/ RSVP\nAn evening of performances that consider the value of liveness and the spectre of commodification. Presented with Roulette.\n“Let’s say live performance art is some kind of canary in the coal mine\,” the late artist Pope.L wrote in 2011. He described the institutionalization of “a form that lives and dies on liveness\,” with museums becoming purveyors of experiences as much as stewards of artworks. “What is its death trying to tell us?” he asked. The fate of liveness is central to\nOn Value\n(2015)\, a book on the relationship between performance and property\, edited by Triple Canopy and Ralph Lemon. In advance of the publication of a second\, expanded edition in early 2027\, we’ve asked a number of artists\, writers\, and performers to make use of the book in considering the value of liveness and the ideology of ownership.\nOn Value\ncenters on the particular experience—as old as the commercialization and circulation of Black art in the United States—of being valued as a source of entertainment or art\, transcendence or truth\, while being discounted as a person. Reflecting on the efforts of institutions to collect\, codify\, and reproduce performance\, the book considers the tensions between objects and bodies\, presence and permanence. For Every A-Side Has a B-Side\, Sol Cabrini\, Jonathan Gonzaléz\, Nile Harris\, Taja Cheek (L’Rain)\, T. Lax\, and yuniya edi kwon will channel the book’s concerns into a variety of performances and readings. Lemon will introduce the event.\nThis event is part of the\n2026 Triple Canopy Symposium\n\, presented in partnership with Critical Minded\n.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nRalph Lemon\nis a choreographer\, writer\, and visual artist based in Brooklyn and Philadelphia. Recent solo exhibitions include “From Out of Space” at Paula Cooper Gallery in 2026 and “Ceremonies Out of the Air”at MoMA PS1 in 2024.\nSol Cabrini\nis an artist\, scholar\, and author of\nTgirl.jpg\n(Roof Books\, 2024)) and\nPoetics of Unbeing\n\, forthcoming from Duke University Press. Her practice addresses the various ways creolization performs and operates in the world.\nTaja Cheek\nis a Brooklyn-born curator and musician based in New York City. A multi-instrumentalist\, she makes music as L’Rain. She is currently the artistic director of Performance Space New York.\nJonathan González\nis a choreographer\, artist\, and writer. They are a 2025 Pew Fellow and 2024 Herb Alpert Award recipient\, and their first book\,\nWays to Move: Black Insurgent Grammars\n\, was published by Ugly Duckling Presse in 2025.\nNile Harris\nis a performer and director of live art. He has done a few things and hopes to do a few more\, God willing.\nyuniya edi kwon\nis a composer-improviser\, multi-instrumentalist\, and interdisciplinary performance maker based in New York City. Her practice connects sound\, movement\, and ceremony to explore transformation and transgression\, ritual practice as a tool to queer ancestral lineage\, and the use of mythology to connect\, obscure\, and reveal.\nT. Lax\nis Curator of Media and Performance at the Museum of Modern Art. Lax has collaborated with Ralph Lemon on exhibitions including “1856 Cessna Road” (The Studio Museum in Harlem\, 2012) and “Ceremonies Out of the Air” (MoMA PS1\, 2024).
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261114T010000Z
SUMMARY:Triple Canopy Symposium 2026 (Day 1)
UID:0ff26844-9fd3-46c8-a2e9-1d2c7660b788@0ff2.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/triple-canopy-symposium-2026-day-1/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nA new live solo performance emerging from the world of the COIN COIN project moves between memory\, imagination\, and lived experience.\n\nEmerging from the world of their ongoing\nCOIN COIN\nproject\, composer\, saxophonist\, and sound experimentalist\nMatana Roberts\npresents a new live solo performance piece that moves between memory\, imagination\, and lived experience. Part story\, part call\, the work follows association rather than chronology with language\, gesture\, silence\, humor\, and chance shaping each telling.\nPart of the lineage of the\nCOIN COIN\nproject was conceived in its inception at Roulette. This work sits at its tail end as a celebratory loving postscript: an underbelly\, making space for ideas and questions that lie beyond the scope of the recordings.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nMatana Roberts\n(they/them) is a dynamic instrumentalist and multidisciplinary artist whose practice explores the mystical roots and intuitive\, spirit-raising traditions of American creative expression. Their innovative work has forged new conceptual approaches to considering narrativity\, history\, and political expression within improvisatory structures. More information is available at\nmatanaroberts.com\n.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261112T010000Z
SUMMARY:Matana Roberts Presents: “off the record…”
