BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\nGuitarist Derek Gripper conjures an ancient South African musical heritage interpreting kora (21-string harp-lute) compositions on solo guitar.\n\nSouth Africa’s master guitarist\nDerek Gripper\nmagically conjures anew a centuries-old ancient\nSouth African musical heritage interpreting\nkora\n(21-string harp-lute) compositions on solo\nguitar. It was 15 years ago that he did what everyone said was impossible: he recorded the works\nof Mali’s great Toumani Diabate on solo classical guitar. Since then\, he has produced nearly 30\nalbums ranging from Diabate to Bach to Arvo Part to Keith Jarrett-style improvisations\, and\ncollaborated with Diabate\, Ballake Sissoko\, and classical guitar legend John Williams.\nBy transcribing and recording Diabate\, Derek argued that the word “classical” should belong to\nAfrica as easily as it belongs to Europe. In this program\, Derek features\nkumbengo\n\, the cyclical\nvariations that sit at the heart of the West African tradition. These small\, ancient kernels of music\nfocus the mind\, soar to melodic heights\, move the body\, and ache the heart. Toumani Diabate\nonce showed the world that the virtuosity of Bach and modern jazz could live inside the Malian\nkora tradition. On Derek’s current tour\, the same richness arrives on a single guitar.\n“It’s hard to imagine a more impressive and passionate rendering of Malian music on classical\nguitar.” – Afropop Worldwide\n“a staggering achievement on solo guitar.” – Songlines Magazine\nPresented by Robert Browning Associates and Lotus Music & Dance in partnership with Roulette. Made possible in part with a grant from the Howard Gilman Foundation.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20270314T010000Z
SUMMARY:Derek Gripper: Music from the Strings of Mali (RBA)
UID:c717bfb4-7bc5-4cd9-a73a-0321f9ad3025@c717.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/derek-gripper-music-from-the-strings-of-mali-rba/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nNew quartet explores acoustic-electronic boundaries\, opened with an immersive solo performance of experimental tap dancing.\n\nCecilia Lopez premieres new compositions written for a fresh quartet of exceptional long-time and recent collaborators. Together\, the quartet explores the boundary between acoustic and electronic sound. Through live amplification and processing\, instrumental timbres are altered\, twisted\, and blended into vivid aural hallucinations where individual voices lose themselves in a collective network of sound.\nIngrid Laubrock\nsaxophones\nTrevor Dunn\ndouble bass\nDan Peck\ntuba\nCecilia Lopez\ncomposition\, electronics\, and processing\nOpening the night is\nMelissa Almaguer\nwith a solo set of tap dance and percussion. Her work explores the layered nature of listening and the ways sound shapes our experience of place and time. Beginning with tap dance and expanding into gongs\, seed percussion\, field recordings\, resonant objects\, and electronics\, Almaguer builds evolving sonic environments where movement\, texture\, and rhythm become forms of storytelling through improvisation.\n“One of the most compelling percussionists to hit the New York scene in recent years isn’t found onstage behind a drum kit\, set of congas or any instrument played with sticks or hands: Melissa Almaguer is a tap-dancer\, whose performances recall Whitney Balliett’s characterization of legendary jazz dancer Baby Laurence that he was “more a drummer than a dancer.”\n–\nHank Shteamer JGI New York\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nCecilia Lopez\nis a composer\, musician and multimedia artist from Buenos Aires\, Argentina currently based in New York. She works across the media of performance\, sound\, installation\, and the creation of sound devices and systems. Her work has been performed and exhibited at Museo de Arte Moderno de Buenos Aires (AR)\, Center for Contemporary Arts (Vilnius\, Lithuania)\, Roulette Intermedium\, Issue Project Room\, Ostrava Days Festival 2011 (Ostrava\, Czech Republic)\, MATA Festival 2012\, among others. Grants and awards include a New York State Council for the Arts Individual project grant sponsored by Harvestworks\, the Media Arts Grant (MAAF) for Individual Artists from the New York State Council for the Arts in partnership with Wave Farm\, the Foundation for Contemporary Arts Emergency Grant\, the Jerome Foundation and Roulette Intermedium Commission and the MacCracken Fellowship from the Graduate School of Arts and Sciences at  New York University. In 2019\, Lopez curated the intermedia festival Folly Systems co-produced by Roulette Intermedium and Outpost Artists Resources that featured 11 international artists. Past and current collaborators include Ingrid Laubrock\, Zeena Parkins\, Carmen Baliero\, Aki Onda\, John Driscoll\, Carrie Schneider and Lars Laumann among others. She holds an MFA in Music/Sound from The Milton Avery School of the Arts Bard College and an MA in composition from Wesleyan University. She is currently a PhD Candidate in composition at the GSAS at NYU University.\nIngrid Laubrock\nIngrid Laubrock is an experimental saxophonist and composer interested in exploring the borders between musical realms and creating multi-layered\, dense and often evocative sound worlds. A prolific composer\, Laubrock was named a “true visionary” by pianist and The Kennedy Center’s Artistic Director Jason Moran\, and a“fully committed saxophonist and visionary” by The New Yorker. Her composition\nVogelfreiwas\nnominated “one of the best 25 Classical tracks of 2018” by The New York Times. Laubrock has performed with Anthony Braxton\, Muhal Richard Abrams\, Jason Moran\, Kris Davis\, Nels Cline\, Tyshawn Sorey\, Tomeka Reid\, Mary Halvorson\, Myra Melford\, Zeena Parkins\, Tom Rainey\, Tim Berne\, Dave Douglas\, Wet Ink\, and many others. Laubrock has composed for ensembles ranging from solo to chamber orchestra. Awards include the 2025 German Jazz Prize\, Fellowship in Jazz Composition by the Arts Foundation\, BBC Jazz Prize for Innovation\, SWR German Radio Jazz Prize\, and German Record Critics Quarterly Award. She won the ‘Downbeat Annual Critics Poll”as Rising Star Soprano Saxophonist (2015) and Tenor Saxophonist (2018) as well as Rising Star Composer and Large Ensemble (2025). Ingrid Laubrock is part-time faculty at Columbia University and The New School. She holds an MFA in Music Composition from Vermont College of Fine Arts.\nTrevor Dunn\nwas born in 1968 behind the Redwood Curtain in Eureka\, CA.  His first instrument was the clarinet and he began to focus on the electric bass at 13.  Four years later he co-founded the avant-rock band Mr. Bungle. In 1990\, he received a BA in music from Humboldt State where he performed Koussevitsky’s\nConcerto for Double Bass\nwith the HSO.  In 2001\, he was commissioned by the Elaine Kaufman Cultural Center to write\nDépaysemant\nfor prepared solo contrabass performed later that year at Merkin Hall\, Lincoln Center\, NYC. Dunn has lived in Brooklyn\, NY  since 2000 and can be heard on over 150 recordings including original film music\nFour Films\n(Tzadik)\, his rock band\nMadLove\n(Ipecac)\, and a collection of chamber music\,\nNocturnes\n(Tzadik)\, featuring a string quartet and solo piano pieces.  He can be heard with John Zorn\, Endangered Blood\, Nels Cline Singers\, Tomahawk\, Kris Davis\, Dan Weiss\, and The Melvins with whom in 2012 he toured all 50 States in 50 days. During the pandemic Dunn founded an independent record label Riverworm Records and released his project SpermChurch\, a duo with Dutch-Indonesian electronic musician Sannety.  His third trio-convulsant record\nSèances\n(Pyroclastic)\, amended with a chamber quartet\, was released in 2022.  Other recent releases include more film music\, KingDunn (acoustic duo with Buzz Osborne from the Melvins)\, Ahleuchatistas and Titan To Tachyons.\nDan Peck\nis a tuba player based in NYC. His tuba\, bass\, and percussion trio The Gate\, with Tom Blancarte and Brian Osborne\, has recorded ten albums and toured Europe three times. Earth Tongues is a trumpet\, tuba\, and percussion trio with Joe Moﬀett and Carlo Costa that has recorded five albums and toured domestically. Past collaborators include Anthony Braxton\, Ingrid Laubrock\, Premature Burial (a trio with Peter Evans and Matt Nelson) and Blood of the Stars (duo with violinist Erica Dicker)\, as well as multiple appearances on the Clean Feed\, Firehouse 12 and ugEXPLODE record labels. Dan releases his music and other friends’ stuff on his label Tubapede Records.\nMelissa Almaguer\nis a Mexico-born\, New York City–based multidisciplinary artist whose primary instrument is tap dance. Her practice explores the relationship between movement\, sound\, and perception\, drawing from surrealism\, natural soundscapes\, and the layered textures of everyday listening. Expanding tap through gongs\, drums\, seed percussion\, resonant objects\, and field recordings\, she creates immersive sonic environments where rhythm\, texture\, and movement continuously shape one another. Almaguer has collaborated with artists including Moor Mother\, William Parker\, Joel Ross\, David Virelles\, Patricia Brennan\, and the Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra\, as well as tap artists Dormeshia\, Derick K. Grant\, Jason Samuels Smith\, Michela Marino Lerman\, and Jumaane Taylor. A recipient of the 2025 Next Jazz Legacy\, 2024 M³ Mutual Mentorship for Musicians\, and 2023 This Is A Movement: Collective Imagining\, her work has been presented at venues including Lincoln Center\, The Joyce Theater\, National Sawdust\, Joe’s Pub\, and The Jazz Gallery.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261104T010000Z
SUMMARY:Cecilia Lopez’s Filter Four // Melissa Almaguer
UID:1e1c5c76-e72c-474a-8992-57b898777c30@1e1c.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/cecilia-lopez-trevor-dunn-dan-peck-ingrid-laubrock/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\nA musical journey combining Western jazz\, ambient music\, American folk\, and traditional Indian classical music.\n\nHammered dulcimer virtuoso\nMax ZT\n\,\nsantoor\n(zither) master\nKunal Gunjal\n\, and\nAmit Kavthekar\n\, a top-ranking\ntabla\n(drums) player of his generation\, unite in a groundbreaking and unprecedented musical journey combining Western jazz\, ambient music\, American folk\, and traditional Indian classical music.\nPresented by Robert Browning Associates and Lotus Music & Dance in partnership with Roulette.\nGrammy-nominated\nMax ZT\n\, whose sound draws from deep study with the legendary Shivkumar Sharma in India and the Cissoko griot family in Senegal\, is known for his innovative technique and global approach\; he has recorded extensively beyond genres.\nKunal Gunjal\n\, a Global Music award-winning artist and featured musician on Oscar-winning films\, was a longtime disciple of the great Shivkumar Sharma with whom he performed over the years.\nAmit Kavthekar\nstudied with Alla Rakha and Zakir Hussain\, and has played with many eminent Indian musicians\, including Shivkumar Sharma\, Amjad Ali Khan and Shahid Parvez\, and recently toured with jazz and world music guitarist Al Di Meola.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261017T000000Z
SUMMARY:Max ZT\, Kunal Gunjal\, and Amit Kavthekar: Strings of Soul (RBA)
UID:3de1bb3e-c5ed-4913-a664-30bcaffdc707@3de1.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/max-zt-kunal-gunjal-and-amit-kavthekar-strings-of-soul/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $30\nPurchase Tickets\nAn improvised structure for an orchestra of voices\, electric guitars\, and an audience\, using the Bill of Rights as its source material.\n\nIn advance of the midterm elections composer/improviser Carla Kihlstedt and poet Rafael Osés present\nX AMENDMENTS\n\,\nan improvised sonic structure for an orchestra of voices\, electric guitars\, and an audience\, u\nsing the first ten amendments of the United States Constitution – The Bill of Rights – as its source material. The performance is an experiment in individual and collective freedoms and an exploration of rights\, responsibilities\, agency\, and restrictions.\nAs Carla Kihlstedt describes it:\n“The eleven performers live as a community and explore what it means to make choices independently\, choices together — how to tell stories\, how to use these clues to make resonances together.”\nHolland Andrews\nvoice\nGelsey Bell\nvoice\nCarla Kihlstedt\nvoice\nAnaïs Maviel\nvoice\nKalyn Harewood\nvoice\nRafael Osés\nvoice\nDither:\nTaylor Levine\nguitar\nJoshua Lopes\nguitar\nJames Moore\nguitar\nBrendon Randall-Myers\nguitar\nBrandon Ross\nguitar\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nCarla Kihlstedt\nis an American composer\, improviser\, writer and educator who plays violin/viola and sings\, often simultaneously. She loves the economy\, intimacy\, and unapologetic directness of song. In her larger pieces (\nBlack Inscription\, Necessary Monsters\, 26 Little Deaths\, At Night We Walk in Circles and Are Consumed by Fire\,\nPandæmonium\, X Amendments\, 6 Dioramas) she explores the complexity of a single idea through many different lenses. Carla is a founding member of the experimental metal band Sleepytime Gorilla Museum\, the composers collective Tin Hat (formerly Tin Hat Trio)\, the song-based projects Rabbit Rabbit Radio and 2 Foot Yard\, and the improvised bands Causing A Tiger\, and Minamo. She is on the faculty of the Contemporary Musical Arts Department of the New England Conservatory\, the MFA in Music Composition program at the Vermont College of Fine Arts at CalArts\, and she co-facilitated the Creative Gesture Lab for composers and choreographers at the Banff Centre for Arts and Creativity. She is currently incubating two new choral projects: Long for This World—an educational\, environmentally-centered youth chorus project\; Feral Choir—an experimental vocal group rooted in the physicality of singing bodies.\nDither\n\, a New York based electric guitar quartet\, is dedicated to an eclectic mix of experimental repertoire which spans composed\, improvised\, and electronic music. Formed in 2007\, the quartet has performed across the United States and abroad\, presenting new commissions\, original compositions\, multimedia works\, and large guitar ensemble pieces. Dither’s members are Taylor Levine\, Joshua Lopes\, James Moore\, and Brendon Randall-Myers.\nHolland Andrews\n(they/them) is a vocalist\, composer\, producer\, and performer whose work focuses on the abstraction of operatic and extended-technique voice to build cathartic and dissonant soundscapes. Andrews arranges music for voice\, clarinet\, and electronics and frequently highlights themes surrounding vulnerability and healing. Andrews harnesses these instruments’ innate qualities of power and elegance to serve as a cohesive vessel for these themes. As a vocalist\, their influences stem from a dynamic range including contemporary opera\, theater\, and jazz\, while also cultivating their own unique vocal style which integrates these influences with language disintegration\, vocal distortion\, and environmental ambience. Andrews previously performed solo music under the stage name Like a Villain. Their previous work also includes composing film scores for Rebeca Huntt’s BEBA (nominated for an Independent Spirit Award and acquired by Neon and Onyx Collective/Hulu) and Sophie Compton and Reuben Hamlyn’s Another Body (awarded SXSW 2023 Documentary Feature Special Jury Award for Innovation in Storytelling). Andrews has gained recognition from publications such as The Wire\, The New York Times\, Vogue\, Le Monde\, and BBC Radio. Holland Andrews is currently based in Brooklyn\, New York.\nGelsey Bell\n(she/they) is a Brooklyn-based multidisciplinary performance creator\, composer\, and vocalist. Her recent works include the experimental opera\nmɔɹnɪŋ [morning//mourning]\n(2023)\, commissioned by the HERE Arts Center and presented in the Prototype Festival\;\nCairns\n(2020)\, a soundwalk for Green-Wood Cemetery\; the musical one-act “Archaeopteris” (2025)\, commissioned by Wet Ink\; and thingNY’s collaboratively written opera\nMouthful\n(2024). She is the Co-Artistic Director of thingNY and Varispeed. She has released multiple albums\, including the recent\nmɔɹnɪŋ [morning//mourning]\n\,\nSkylighght\n\,\nand\nHeads Together\n. She has received awards from the Foundation for Contemporary Arts\, Opera America\, the Aaron Copland Fund for Music\, the Japan Foundation\, NYSCA\, and others. Performance highlights also include Dave Malloy’s\nNatasha\, Pierre\, & the Great Comet of 1812\n(Broadway) and\nGhost Quartet\n\, Robert Ashley’s\nCelestial Excursions\nand\nForeign Experiences\n\, Darius Jones’s\nSamesoul Maker\n\, Alaina Ferris’s\nThe Lydian Gale Parr\n\, Aaron Siegel’s\nRainbird\n\, and other works by Jay Afrisando\, Kate Soper\, Tomomi Adachi\, Brent Michael Davids\, and others.\nAnaïs Maviel\nis a multidisciplinary artist whose work transforms spiritual concepts into immersive sensory experience\, treating perception itself as a medium. A composer\, vocalist\, and multi-instrumentalist\, she works across traditional and experimental practices to explore the power of sound to shape reality and highlight the stakes of cultural multiplicity. She moves fluidly between song\, choral\, instrumental\, and orchestral music\, as well as interactive staging\, drawing on sacred geometry and cosmologies of sound as structural foundations.\nThrough an aesthetically layered vocabulary and embodied processes\, she creates multidimensional\, “spiritually illuminating” experiences (\nNew York City Jazz Record\n). Her expansive collaborations across mediums include Alarm Will Sound\, Craig Taborn\, Meshell Ndegeocello\, Meredith Monk\, Merce Cunningham Trust\, Okwui Okpokwasili\, Sean Webley\, and Nene Humphrey.\nJazz Right Now has called her a “unique aesthetic visionary.” She is a recipient of the Van Lier Fellowship (2019)\, the American Composers Forum Create Award (2020)\, the Jerome Foundation Fellowship (2021–2022)\, the New York Foundation for the Arts Artists Fellowship (2022)\, New Music USA’s Next Jazz Legacy (2023)\, and a nominee for the Herb Alpert Award in Music Composition (2023).
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261009T000000Z
SUMMARY:X AMENDMENTS: Carla Kihlstedt\, Rafael Osés\, Holland Andrews\, Gelsey Bell\, Anaïs Maviel\, Kalyn Harewood\, and Dither (Night 2)
UID:d7da4162-cd73-4ee6-945b-04382389ee59@d7da.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/x-amendments-carla-kihlstedt-rafael-oses-dither-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nFor his Roulette Commission\, Naqvi performs two new works for modular synthesizer and chamber ensemble.\n\nFor his Roulette Commission\,\nQasim Naqvi\nwill perform two new works—a solo study for modular synthesizer and a chamber work for piano\, string trio\, and three improvisers\, featuring\nCaroline Davis\n\,\nGrey Mcmurray\,\nScott Colberg\,\nand members of the\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble.\nA note from Naqvi on the inspiration behind the work:\n“In 2015\, I was invited to create music for a video installation\, and through that project I began to study the music of New York composer Earle Brown and the early work of the Polish composer Krzysztof Penderecki. What drew me to both composers was their unique approach to open form and graphic notational systems—a symbolic notational language that allowed for broad interpretive possibilities within an ensemble. When infused with traditional musical notation and very concise parameters\, the result for me was a kind of magical tightrope between legible and illegible musical form. Like an image distorting and rippling back into its natural state. Through this study\, I created a series of works utilizing a graphic notational system of my own design. This evening’s music is a continuation of that language\, which I am revisiting after almost a decade.\nThese pieces are dedicated to Roulette Intermedium. Creative music institutions have risen and fallen over the decades\, yet Roulette has persevered\, programming some of the most uncompromising and adventurous art in the world. This music is a dedication to their spirit.”\nQasim Naqvi\nmodular synthesizer\nCaroline Davis\nalto saxophone\nGrey Mcmurray\nprepared acoustic guitar\, electric guitar\nScott Colberg\nacoustic bass\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble\nJosh Modney\nviolin\nMiranda Cuckson\nviola\nMichael Nicholas\ncello\nErika Dohi\npiano\n*FREE FOR ALL ROULETTE MEMBERS *\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nQasim Naqvi\nis a percussionist\, composer\, and synthesist. In addition to being the drummer of the lauded cult minimalist trio Dawn of Midi\, Naqvi is an accomplished solo artist whose passion for multidisciplinary work has led him into the worlds of film\, dance\, installation art\, and orchestral and chamber music. His concert music has been commissioned and performed by London Contemporary Orchestra\, BBC Concert Orchestra\, Crash Ensemble\, Bang on a Can All-Stars\, Jennifer Koh\, Stargaze\, Cello Octet Amsterdam\, Helsinki Chamber Choir\, among others. Other collaborations include original music for Academy Award–winning documentarian Laura Poitras and Turner Prize–nominated filmmaker and installation artist Naeem Mohaiemen. Qasim has released music as a bandleader on the labels Erased Tapes Records\, New Amsterdam Records\, Red Hook Records\, and NNA Tapes.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261119T010000Z
SUMMARY:Qasim Naqvi: Slow Television
UID:82b016da-de86-46ef-96f3-58a95f39d773@82b0.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/qasim-naqvi-slow-television/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $15\nPurchase Tickets\nA one-night\, multi-channel video installation for site-specific performance.\n\nEmanate / Crenellate\nby\nTrouble\n(\nSam Hillmer\nand\nLaura Paris\n) is a one-night\, sculptural and interactive multi-channel video installation for site-specific performance. The environment will be inhabited by a host of esteemed experimentalists\, with the audience roaming free rather than seated. Restructuring habits of assembly has been the thematic driver of Trouble’s two-decade career\;\nEmanate / Crenellate\ncontinues that work into digital space.\nAntenes\nLuke Stewart\nPatrick Higgins\nDome Theater\nDJ sets by R. Gamble x Heidi Sabertooth (Lost Soul Enterprises)\n“It was not Trouble’s goal to complicate the performance experience so much as it was to encounter it from it’s own inbuilt interactivity”\n-Warren Fry\, The Brooklyn Rail\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nTrouble\n(2006-present) is the collaborative identity of Sam Hillmer and Laura Paris who live and work in New York City. Their conceptual and polysensory practice spans installation\, sculpture\, performance\, video\, new media and social practice. Trouble’s work focuses on boundaries between social ritual and art.\nIntricate life size maze sculptures are central to Trouble’s practice. Sprawling and complex in nature\, these multimedia maze installations experiment with space and audience experience\, testing the capacity of maze structure to interrupt habits of assembly. Site specific performance programs curated by Trouble are presented inside each of Trouble’s maze installations. Trouble has garnered attention for the visceral audience response to their maze designs: fostering temporary loss of agency by inhibiting audience mobility\, Trouble’s maze work provokes psychic states that hover between ecstatic and exasperated.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261024T000000Z
SUMMARY:Trouble x Diamond Terrifier: Emanate / Crenellate with Antenes\, Luke Stewart\, Patrick Higgins\, Dome Theater\, and DJ sets by R. Gamble & Heidi Sabertooth
UID:d76f59af-d9f4-494e-89ae-e170b778142b@d76f.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/trouble-emanate-crenellate-sam-hillmer-laura-paris/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $20\nPurchase Tickets\nThe Interpretations series returns with sets by acclaimed pianist Ursel Schlicht and Jason Kao Hwang's jazz trio.\n\nThe Interpretations Series continues its 37th season at Roulette with a solo performance by pianist\nUrsel Schlicht\nand a set from the jazz trio Critical Response\, featuring\nJason Kao Hwang\non violin and viola\,\nAnders Nilsson\non guitar\, and\nMichael T.A. Thompson\non drums and percussion.\nPresented by Interpretations in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nThe Interpretations series\nis a New York-based concert series focusing on the relationship between contemporary composers and their interpreters. Sometimes the interpreters are the composers themselves\; more often\, the series features performers who specialize in the interpretation of new music. Since its inception in 1989\, Interpretations has featured leading figures in contemporary music and multimedia\, including Muhal Richard Abrams\, Robert Ashley\, Anthony Braxton\, Thomas Buckner\, FLUX Quartet\, Joseph Kubera\, Annea Lockwood\, and Alvin Lucier\, Roscoe Mitchell\, Phill Niblock\, Pauline Oliveros\, Ursula Oppens\, and Morton Subotnick.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261023T000000Z
SUMMARY:Interpretations: Ursel Schlicht // Critical Response
UID:e29a83ce-27b5-44ba-a91e-eefe8a69f2c7@e29a.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/interpretations-ursel-schlicht-critical-response/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nThree performers collide\, spin\, fall\, and intertwine in an athletic maze of child’s play and menacing acts.\n\nHow Dogs Play\nexplores the complex presence of aggression in intimacy\, excavating the seen and unseen ruptures between self and other. In a tumult of childlike play\, rivalrous antics\, and feats of maniacal athleticism\, this work tunes the dial of intensity to animate an underworld of feeling. We work\, dare\, push the edge\, cross that line\, slipping between human\, animal\, and monster. This dance is for the State Champ\, the Junkie\, the Hustler\, those who push their bodies into the unknown\, chasing the sensational and the spiritual.\nAmelia Heintzelman\nchoreography & performance\nBrendan Elefante\, Jack Dexter\nperformance\nTommy Martinez\nsound\nTess Michaelson\ndramaturgy\nSylvie Hayes-Wallace\ncostume\n“Amelia Heintzelman charged the space with a burst of energy wavering formidably between violence and vulnerability.” –Gia Kourlas\, New York Times\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nAmelia Heintzelman\nis a dancer\, choreographer\, and teacher. Her work has been supported by residencies\, commissions\, and grants through Center for Performance Research\, Draftwork at Danspace Project\, Foundation for Contemporary Arts\, Issue Project Room\, Movement Research at the Judson Church\, New York Foundation for the Arts\, Pageant\, Snug Harbor\, and UCross Foundation. Most recently\, she’s worked as a collaborating performer for Kim Brandt\, Jesi Cook\, Ayano Elson\, Deborah Hay\, Melinda Ring and Alexa West. She is on teaching faculty at Movement Research and Pageant\, and rarely/sometimes teaches Comedy Pilates.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261202T010000Z
SUMMARY:Amelia Heintzelman: How Dogs Play (Night 1)
UID:addf650e-c5b1-4243-a66b-b620ac40739a@addf.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/amelia-heintzelman-how-dogs-play/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $20\nPurchase Tickets\nThe Interpretations Series returns with two ensembles — a percussion\, flute\, sax\, and synth quartet\, and a piano\, percussion\, and bass trio.\n\nThe Interpretations Series continues its 37th season at Roulette with a double bill featuring two ensembles:\nGerry Hemingway\npercussion\nLina Andonovska\nflutes\nEarl Howard\nsaxophone\, synthesizer\n+ Special guest\nKyle Motl\ndouble bass\nEli Wallace\npiano\nNick Neuburg\ndrums\, percussion\nPresented by Interpretations in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nThe Interpretations series\nis a New York-based concert series focusing on the relationship between contemporary composers and their interpreters. Sometimes the interpreters are the composers themselves\; more often\, the series features performers who specialize in the interpretation of new music. Since its inception in 1989\, Interpretations has featured leading figures in contemporary music and multimedia\, including Muhal Richard Abrams\, Robert Ashley\, Anthony Braxton\, Thomas Buckner\, FLUX Quartet\, Joseph Kubera\, Annea Lockwood\, and Alvin Lucier\, Roscoe Mitchell\, Phill Niblock\, Pauline Oliveros\, Ursula Oppens\, and Morton Subotnick.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261204T010000Z
SUMMARY:Interpretations: Gerry Hemingway\, Lina Andonovska\, and Earl Howard // Kyle Motl\, Eli Wallace\, and Nick Neuburg
UID:2e4acf59-2d04-4ee7-85b3-6186b361ad34@2e4a.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/hemingway-andonovska-howard-motl-wallace-neuburg/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nJazz Generations Initiative Continuum Fellow presents a three night residency featuring poets\, musicians\, and artists across generations.\n\n“The artists represented tonight I’ve admired for a long time. I’ve dreamed of a bill just focusing on their solo work and it’s finally happening. Playing solo is no easy feat: There’s nothing to fall back on\, and it’s the most intimate musical experience — direct communication with the audience.” — James Brandon Lewis\nAward-winning saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\n\, a\nJazz Generations Initiative (JGI)\nContinuum Fellow\n\, presents\nThru Line\n\, a three-night residency at Roulette as part of his 2026 fellowship. The series brings together improvisers\, poets\, musicians\, and multidisciplinary artists from across media and generations.\nThis evening’s performance will feature a series of solo sets by\nJames Brandon Lewis\n\, flutist/composer\nNicole Mitchell\n\, cellist/vocalist\nJanel Leppin\n\, singer/producer\nJenna Camille\n\, trombonist/composer\nKalia Vandever\n\, Grammy-winning sound chemist\nVal Jeanty\,\ntransdisciplinary artist\nShara Lunon\,\nand pianist/composer\nAlexis Marcelo\n.\nThe night will conclude with a collective improvisation featuring all of the artists.\n“A Saxophonist Who Embodies and Transcends Tradition” — The NY Times\n“James Brandon Lewis does not take the easy road. Having forged a singular sound on the tenor saxophone\, he could simply devise settings that showcase his brawny tone. Instead\, he has rooted his recent music in extramusical research.” — DownBeat\nPresented by the Jazz Generations Initiative in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nThe JGI Continuum Fellowship\nconvenes a cohort of New York-based early-to mid-career musicians and multidisciplinary artists to curate concerts and programs that foster creative exchange across artistic disciplines and generations.\nTenor saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\nhas emerged as a leading figure on the improvised music scene in New York City and across the world. The winner of seven DownBeat magazine awards (including “artist of the year” in the 2025 critics’ poll)\, and a 2026 Guggenheim Fellowship\, Lewis has led ensembles on some of the most prominent stages across the world\, from the Village Vanguard to the Bimhuis Muziekgebouw. He has received accolades from The New York Times\, NPR Music and countless other outlets.The late Sonny Rollins once told Lewis\, “When I listen to you\, I listen to Buddha\, I listen to Confucius… I listen to the deeper meaning of life. You are keeping the world in balance.” Lewis relocated to New York City in 2012\, and since then he has become known as one of the leading figures on the scene\, uniting his background in gospel and jazz with an interest in Western classical music\, hip-hop\, punk rock\, other music traditions from around the globe\, and art forms outside of music. His celebrated projects include the James Brandon Lewis Trio\, the American Book Award-winning Heroes Are Gang Leaders\, the Red Lily Quintet\, and his ongoing collaboration with the Messthetics\, which has yielded two critically acclaimed albums on Impulse! Records. Lewis\, who recently earned a PhD in Creativity from Rowan University in New Jersey\, also works across mediums as a writer and poet.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261016T000000Z
SUMMARY:Art of Solo: James Brandon Lewis\, Nicole Mitchell\, Janel Leppin\, Jenna Camille\, Kalia Vandever\, Val Jeanty\, Shara Lunon\, and Alexis Marcelo
UID:55ccd321-cd14-4b19-81b9-a71edd9432b2@55cc.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/james-brandon-lewis-art-of-solo/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nTwo jazz legends present a rare duo concert\, putting to fore the consummate magic of their deeply developed communion.\n\nMatthew Shipp and William Parker present a rare duo concert celebrating their boundless four-decade collaboration. Their new studio album\,\nA Whole System Of\n—their fourth duo recording—will be released on November 6 via TAO Forms. On the same day\, their first duo album\,\nZo\n\, will be reissued by ESP-Disk’\, full circle style.\nThe intimacy of the duo will wholly feature the consummate magic of their deeply developed communion.\nMatthew Shipp\npiano\nWilliam Parker\nbass\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nMatthew Shipp\nis recognized for having forged a unique pianistic language within an expansive body of work that bridges the avant-garde tradition with 21st-century sonic landscapes\, expanding the expressive possibilities of jazz in the process.\nA renowned master of the double bass\,\nWilliam Parker\nis also recognized for his lifelong dedication to free jazz as a spiritual practice and community-building force — work that has ensured the continuity of an essential art form and fostered a supportive ecosystem for generations of creative musicians.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261205T010000Z
SUMMARY:Matthew Shipp & William Parker: A Whole System Of
UID:bfb50540-e7d3-407e-a554-870d9f69548e@bfb5.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/matthew-shipp-william-parker-duo-a-whole-system-of/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nOne of the longest-standing musicians’ associations in the United States celebrates 61 years of fostering creative music.\n\nAssociation for the Advancement of Creative Musicians’s (AACM) New York City Chapter\nhosts a two-night celebration of their 61st anniversary\, with Richarda Abrams as the Mistress of Ceremonies.\nJD PARRAN & SPIRIT STAGE 3\nChristopher Dean Sullivan\nbass\nAlexis Marcelo\npiano\nMichael T A Thompson\npercussion\nJD Parran\nsaxophone\, clarinet\,  flutes\nADEGOKE STEVE COLSON & IQUA COLSON\nAdegoke Steve Colson\npiano\nIqua Colson\nvocals\nSteven Bernstein\ntrumpet\nLuke Stewart\nbass\nChris Beck\ndrums\nThis evening is presented by AACM New York City Chapter\, Inc. in Partnership with Roulette.\nThe Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians (AACM)\n\, a non-profit tax-exempt arts organization co-founded in 1965 by Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams\, Jodie Christian\, Kelan Phil Cohran\, and Steve McCall\, is one of the oldest musicians associations in the United States. Since its founding in Chicago\, Illinois in 1965\, the organization’s members have developed a body of work that encompasses the spectrum of traditional and contemporary music.\nThe Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians New York City Chapter\, Inc.\, a non-profit tax-exempt entity\, was established in 1983 by AACM members who moved to New York. The AACM is significantly unique in that it has nurtured and brought to fruition so many accomplished composers and performers. The organization’s artists have developed a body of work that has achieved lasting significance across borders of music genres and geography to play a critical role in the ever evolving process of music creation and its vital influence throughout the world. AACM members who have been featured in our programs include: Anthony Braxton\, Roscoe Mitchell\, Wadada Leo Smith\, Henry Threadgill\, Thurman Barker\, Adegoke Steve Colson\, Iqua Colson\, Chico Freeman\, Amina Claudine Myers\, George Lewis\, Reggie Nicholson\, Peggy Abrams\, Richarda Abrams\, Laroy Wallace McMillan and posthumously Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams\, Leonard E. Jones\, Frank Gordon\, Joseph Jarman\, Leroy Jenkins\, Kalaparusha Ahra Difda\, Lester Bowie\, Steve McCall\, Fred Anderson\, John Stubblefield and Fred Hopkins. AACM New York City Chapter\, Inc. continues to produce concerts annually in New York. Our members have received degrees from top Universities and have expertise in many areas ie. artists\, painters\, and novelists. Because of the AACM New York City Chapter\, Inc. our members have gained international recognition as accomplished musicians and composers\, many of whom have been invited by renowned orchestras throughout the USA and Europe to perform their music. Our members are master musician composers and have taught in major universities all over the world.\nRicharda Abrams\nrecorded\nSong for All\n(Black Saint Records) with her father Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams and performed in Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams’ Ensemble at Roulette Intermedium. She is a member of Amina Claudine Myers’ Generation IV\, a gospel group\, Myers’ Voice Choir\, and has toured worldwide. She is a 4X AUDELCO award-winning actress/singer/playwright/producer of\nFirst By Faith: The Life of Mary McLeod Bethune\n\,\nBethune: Our Black Velvet Rose and Big Mama and Me\n. Theatre productions include:\nHaving Our Say: The Delany Sisters First 100 Years\n(East Lynne Theater Co.)\,\nTelling Tales Out of School\n(New Federal Theater dir.Woodie King Jr.)\,\nAmerican Rot\nand\nLast Days of Judas Iscariot\n(La Mama\, dir. Estelle Parsons)\,\nFear Itself\n(Crossroads)\,and\nGuess Who’s Coming to Dinner (\nIvoryton Playhouse). Film/TV:\nThe Subject\n\,\nThe Riverside Bench\n\,\nOreos With Attitude\n(Showtime)\, and\nSesame Street\n.\nJD Parran\njust returned from his hometown\, St. Louis\, where he was featured artist at The Missouri History Museum’s monumental Millcreek Exhibit. JD\, a charter member of BAG (Black Artist Group of St. Louis) was greeted by a large audience of friends and extended family. Parran’s recently released duo recording\,\nCalifornia Street Sessions\nwith Mark Deutsch\, has received critical acclaim. He has performed and/or recorded with James Jabbo Ware\, Hamiet Bluiett\, Melanie Dyer\, Anthony Braxton\, George E. Lewis\, Douglas Ewart\, Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams\, Andrew Drury\, Julius Hemphill\, Oliver Lake\, Marty Ehrlich\, Henry Threadgill\, Adegoke Steve Colson\, Amir Elsaffar\, Stevie Wonder\, Lena Horne\, and Cecil Taylor among many others.\nAdegoke Steve Colson\n&\nIqua Colson\nare referred to as a “musical power couple” by The New York Times (2017) and Downbeat Magazine (2025)\, the music of composer-pianist\nAdegoke Steve Colson\nand composer-vocalist\nIqua Colson\nfocuses on many facets of the human experience. Early members of the influential AACM\, their critically acclaimed performances and recordings often illuminate social issues. Their projects have featured innovators such as Reggie Workman\, Henry Threadgill\, Anthony Davis\, David Murray\, Leroy Jenkins\, Tomeka Reid\, Wallace Roney\, Tyshawn Sorey\, Maia\, Joseph Jarman\, Oliver Lake and Jabali Billy Hart as well as master artists of other disciplines including dancer/choreographer Savion Glover\, writers/activists Amina & Amiri Baraka (LeRoi Jones) and dancer/actress Carmen de Lavallade. They met early ‘70s while they were piano majors at Northwestern University School of Music. They were honored in 2024 when The Northwestern University Libraries developed The Adegoke Steve Colson and Iqua Colson Archives\, including scores\, compositions\, and artifacts relative to the first 50 years of their varied and prolific careers.\nIqua Colson\nis an award winning lyricist who worked with tenor saxophone titan Fred Anderson in her early career in a band that included George Lewis\, Hamid Drake and Douglas Ewart. Her compositions usually center around vocal performances. Ade is a decorated composer who has written for large and small ensembles and received several honors and commissions supporting his work in music composition. He has worked and recorded with such artists as Baikida Carroll\, Andrew Cyrille\, Butch Morris and David Murray. His works for small ensembles have been recorded by such noted artists as bassist Richard Davis\, composer and trumpeter Hannibal\,\nTrioThree\n\, and most recently by Andrew Cyrille on his ECM Release\, “The News.” Trailblazers at the start of their careers\, they created their record label Silver Sphinx & their first release in 1980\, “Triumph!”\, later became part of the influential BBC Broadcaster Gilles Peterson’s 2011 compilation “Freedom\, Rhythm & Sound – Revolutionary Jazz and The Civil Rights Movement”. They’ve been credited as part of the early roots of the “indie” movement alongside such early independent visionaries as Maurice White\, John Coltrane\, Sun Ra\, & Mary Lou Williams. The Colsons latest release “Glow: Music for Trio…Add Voice” featuring Mark Helias and Andrew Cyrille was released late 2025. Adegoke Steve Colson is a Steinway Artist.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261105T010000Z
SUMMARY:AACM NYC: Celebrating 61 Years of Creative Music with JD Parran & Spirit Stage 3 //  Adegoke Steve Colson and Iqua Colson
UID:358ea8d2-9a62-472d-816e-1fc7318ca692@358e.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/aacm-celebrating-61-years-of-creative-music/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nTwo evenings bring together artists representing the radical spirit that continues to shape the landscape of contemporary music.\n\nA two-night mini-festival celebrating the 80th anniversary of the Internationales Musikinstitut Darmstadt’s groundbreaking Summer Course for New Music—the global meeting point for postwar experimentation\, exchange\, and creative risk.\nNight one features the celebrated\nMivos Quartet\n\, the New York City debut of\nGBSR Duo\n\, and guitarist\nYaron Deutsch\n.\nThe festival will also include archival listening sessions and lectures exploring Darmstadt’s historic legacy and its living\, evolving influence on today’s artistic practice. Additional details to be announced.\nMivos Quartet\nOlivia De Prato\nviolin\nWilliam Overcash\nviolin\nVictor Lowrie Tafoya\nviola\nNathan Watts\ncello\nGBSR Duo\nGeorge Barton\npercussion\nSiwan Rhys\npiano\nYaron Deutsch\nguitar\n“Here was a rare immersion in modern classics.”—\nThe New York Times on the 70\nth\nAnniversary celebration of Darmstadt\, presented in 2016 at Roulette\nOrganized by\nNick Hallett\nand\nZach Layton\n\, curators of NYC’s Darmstadt: Classics of the Avant-Garde\, together with\nThomas Schäfer\n\, director of the IMD\, and\nGeorge Lewis\n\, Artistic Director of the International Contemporary Ensemble\, the festival bridges generations of experimental sound. Envisioned in partnership with a consortium including\nRoulette Intermedium\, The New School\, Fridman Gallery\,\nand the\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nMivos Quartet\nis devoted to performing works of contemporary composers and presenting diverse new music to international audiences. The quartet\, founded in 2008\, at the Manhattan School of Music’s Contemporary Performance program\, commissions and premieres new repertoire for string quartet\, and is dedicated to creative collaborations with a wide variety of artists. Mivos maintains an active international performance schedule\, regularly appearing as ensemble-in-residence at festivals including June in Buffalo\, Shanghai New Music Week\, and Wien Modern. Mivos participates in many educational residencies at universities and summer festivals\, working with young performers and composers to develop their skills. The quartet also runs two composition prizes to help discover and promote emerging composers in the US and abroad. Beyond these activities\, Mivos is committed to collaborating with guest artists\, exploring multimedia projects\, and performing improvised music. The Mivos Quartet is Olivia De Prato\, violin\, William Overcash\, violin\, Victor Lowrie Tafoya\, viola\, and Nathan Watts\, cello.\nYaron Deutsch\nis an Israeli guitarist\, improviser\, and artistic director of Ensemble Nikel\, which he co-founded in 2006 with saxophonist Gan Lev. Known for his work in contemporary and experimental music\, Deutsch has collaborated extensively with leading composers including Michael Beil\, Pierluigi Billone\, Raphaël Cendo\, Chaya Czernowin\, Philippe Hurel\, Stefan Prins\, and Michael Wertmüller\, premiering new works as both soloist and ensemble musician. A frequent guest performer with Klangforum Wien\, he has also appeared with Ensemble musikFabrik\, Ensemble Ictus\, the Israel Philharmonic Orchestra\, and major European orchestras under conductors including Peter Eötvös and Zubin Mehta. Deutsch is regularly on the faculty of Darmstadt Summer Course.\nGBSR Duo\nPercussionist George Barton and pianist Siwan Rhys are one of the UK’s leading contemporary chamber ensembles\, celebrated for fearless performances\, ambitious commissions\, and inventive collaborations. Their repertoire spans twentieth-century modernism\, experimental music\, and cross-genre projects\, with programs featuring composers from Karlheinz Stockhausen and Galina Ustvolskaya to Brian Eno and Aphex Twin. The duo has premiered works by Eva-Maria Houben\, Cassie Kinoshi\, Linda Catlin Smith\, and Oliver Leith. They are winners of the Royal Philharmonic Society 2025 Young Artist Award. GBSR has performed internationally with ensembles and artists including the Los Angeles Philharmonic\, Basel Sinfonietta\, and Thomas Adès. In 2026\, they serve as Artists in Residence at Kings Place.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261211T010000Z
SUMMARY:Darmstadt at 80 Festival: Mivos Quartet plays Rebecca Saunders and Herbert Brün // GBSR Duo // Yaron Deutsch
UID:be390482-c300-4ed7-b498-546519fb519d@be39.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/darmstadt-at-80-festival-mivos-quartet-rebecca-saunders-herbert-brun-yaron-deutsch-gbsr-duo/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\n"Queen of the Tar" performs solo and with ensemble for an evening that explores the rich traditions of Persian music.\n\nAcclaimed tar virtuoso Sahba Motallebi\, known as the “Queen of the Tar\,” opens the evening with an extraordinary solo performance. She’ll then be joined by an outstanding ensemble featuring\nMarjan Vahdat\n\, an award-winning vocalist celebrated for her distinctive contemporary interpretation of Persian music\;\nReza Mohsenipour\n\, a tar and bass tar master\, musicologist and composer whose work is devoted to preserving and expanding the rich traditions of Persian classical music\; and\nHamid Mohsenipour\n\, a dynamic percussionist\, flutist\, composer\, and co-founder of the Barbad Project with his brothers Reza and Navid.\n“A virtuoso performer.” – LA Weekly\n“There is no rock guitarist\, no oud player\, possibly no musician anywhere in the world with such subtle yet fearsome chops on a fretted instrument.” – New York Music Daily\nPresented by Robert Browning Associates and Lotus Music & Dance in partnership with Roulette.\nSahba Motallebi\nis recognized internationally for her exquisite performances of Persian music on the tar\, a lute-like stringed instrument central to one of the world’s great musical traditions.  Widely recognized as a master of the Persian tar\, she has dedicated her life to preserving the rich traditions of Persian classical music while expanding its expressive possibilities through original composition and interdisciplinary artistic work. With a career that spans decades\, she has been acclaimed for her solo performances and collaborations with such renowned artists as Hossein Alizadeh\, Mohammad Reza Shajarian\, Yo-Yo Ma\, and Kayhan Kalhor\, and her work on the Grammy-winning\nFandango at the Wall\nalbum.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261106T010000Z
SUMMARY:Sahba Motallebi & Ensemble: Persian Music – an Evening of Solidarity and Harmony (RBA)
UID:3246c3cc-7d9a-47d9-b8a7-49f3abfbaee0@3246.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/sahba-motallebi-persian-rba/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nInteractive sound art pioneer + painter/sculptor celebrate Harvestworks’ 50th\, plus a convo with e-flux Performance Curator Sanna Almajedi.\n\nHarvestworks and Roulette are pleased to co-present\nLive Processing Paint and Sound\n\, a performance by pioneer of interactive sound art\nLiz Phillips\nand painter\nHeidi Howard\n. The program\, presented as part of Harvestworks’ 50th anniversary celebration\, will include an introduction by Harvestworks’ Interim Director\nIvana Dama\n\, followed by a conversation with\nSanna Almajedi\n\, Performance Curator at e-flux.\nThroughout the creation of Heidi Howard’s painting\, Liz Phillips will live-process aquatic recordings using Mugic and light sensors\, contact microphones\, and a theremin\, transforming brushstrokes and fish activity into an immersive water soundscape. Live video synthesis from six camera and screen feeds will illuminate Roulette’s large projection screen.\nLiz Phillips\nsynthesizers and custom electronics\nHeidi Howard\nsensors and live painting\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nLiz Phillips\nis a New York–based artist and a pioneer of interactive sound art whose installations explore the relationships between sound\, technology\, architecture\, and the natural environment. For more than four decades\, she has created immersive works that use custom electronics\, sensors\, and spatial audio to transform audiences into active participants. Inspired by her early experiences in nature along the Hudson River\, Phillips has developed environments in which sound responds to human presence\, redefining the experience of space through interaction. Her work has been exhibited internationally at institutions including the Whitney Museum of American Art\, the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art\, the Walker Art Center\, Ars Electronica\, The Kitchen\, Creative Time\, and the Spoleto Festival USA. She has also collaborated with the Merce Cunningham Dance Company. Phillips is the recipient of a 2024 Anonymous Was A Woman Award and a United States Artists Fellowship\, recognizing her pioneering contributions to sound art and experimental media.\nHeidi Howard\nis a Queens\, New York–based artist whose practice spans portrait painting\, site-specific wall paintings\, sculpture\, and collaborative installations. Working across painting and spatial environments\, Howard explores the relationship between people and their surroundings\, often creating portraits with the sitter present. Their work has been exhibited at Wave Hill\, Gaa Gallery\, the Rhode Island School of Design\, the Southeastern Center for Contemporary Art\, the Queens Museum\, W139\, the Hunterdon Museum of Art\, and James Cohan Gallery. Howard has participated in residencies including the Terra Summer Residency\, Rauschenberg Residency\, Carrizozo AIR\, and Palazzo Monti. Their work has been featured in\nVogue\n\,\nThe Paris Review\n\,\nThe Brooklyn Rail\n\, and other publications. Howard’s first monograph\,\nColors Make Us Do Vibrant Deeds!\n\, was published in conjunction with a solo exhibition at the LeRoy Neiman Center for Print Studies at Columbia University.\nSanna Almajedi\n(b. 1993\, Baghdad) is a curator and writer based in New York\, currently serving as Performance Curator at e-flux and co-curator of the Immigrant Artist Biennial. She recently served as Performance Curator for the 13th Seoul Mediacity Biennale\, Séance: Technology of the Spirit\, held between August and November 2025. Almajedi co-curated Publishing Against the Grain–which was produced by Independent Curators International and toured institutions including Zeitz MOCAA and CCA\, Lagos–and more recently curated Babel at SARA’S / Dunkunsthalle\, Summer Dinner at Slip House\, and Bricks of Memory\, Fragments of Home\, an online exhibition for White Columns.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261029T000000Z
SUMMARY:Live Processing Paint and Sound: Liz Phillips and Heidi Howard
UID:90f26ad7-71d6-43c7-a22a-5a89e541aa4a@90f2.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/live-processing-paint-sound-liz-phillips-heidi-howard/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\nRahul Sharma and Amit Kavthekar pay tribute to two of the greatest musicians in Indian music\n\nTwo extraordinary musicians\,\nsantoor\n(hammered dulcimer) player\nRahul Sharma\nand\ntabla\n(drums) player\nAmit Kavthekar\n\, pay tribute to two of the greatest musicians in\nIndian music in recent years. Shivkumar Sharma\, India’s foremost master of the\nsantoor\, was responsible for developing the santoor from a little known Kashmiri folk\ninstrument into a solo instrument in the classical North Indian repertoire. Tabla maestro\nZakir Hussain\, an international phenomenon\, was one of the world’s most influential\nmusicians and celebrated as the finest percussionist in world music. Both Rahul\nSharma and Amit Kavthekar played with these two legendary artists.\nRahul Sharma\nsantoor\nAmit Kavthekar\ntabla\nPresented by Robert Browning Associates and Lotus Music & Dance in partnership with Roulette\nRahul Sharma\n\, regarded as the leading santoor player of his generation\, has carved a\nniche for himself in the world of Indian classical and fusion music with over 65 released\nalbums in a career of more than 25 years. He has become a worthy successor to his\nfather and guru Shivkumar Sharma\, with whom he studied the santoor. In recent years\nhe received the prestigious Sangeet Natak Akademi Award.\nAmit Kavthekar\nis one of\nthe top-ranking tabla players of his generation. He studied with Alla Rakha and Zakir\nHussain\, and has played with many maestros of Indian classical music\, including Amjad\nAli Khan\, Pandit Jasraj\, Shahid Parvez\, and Vishwa Mohan Bhatt. He frequently\ncollaborates with leading musicians in Western classical music\, jazz and fusion music\,\nand has toured with guitarist Al Di Meola.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20270320T000000Z
SUMMARY:Rahul Sharma & Amit Kavthekar: Tribute to Shivkumar Sharma and Zakir Hussain (RBA)
UID:e99a6071-f3e1-4bdf-874f-687eae735e01@e99a.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/rahul-sharma-amit-kavthekar-tribute-to-shivkumar-sharma-and-zakir-hussain-rba/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nThe 16th consecutive year of Niblock’s meditative installation—numerous video projections soundtracked by his iconic and permeating drone.\n\n6pm-midnight\n*Tickets allow re-entry.*\nAs the longest night of the year unfolds\, Roulette stages Phill Niblock’s annual Winter Solstice concert for the 16th consecutive year\, in collaboration with his partner\, video artist Katherine Liberovskaya.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 6pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nPhill Niblock\n(1933-2024\, USA) was an artist whose fifty-year career spanned minimalist and experimental music\, film\, and photography. Since 1985\, he served as director of Experimental Intermedia\, a foundation for avant-garde music based in New York with a branch in Ghent\, and curator of the foundation’s record label XI. Known for his thick\, loud drones of music\, Niblock’s signature sound is filled with microtones of instrumental timbres that generate many other tones in the performance space. In 2013\, his diverse artistic career was the subject of a retrospective realized in partnership between Circuit (Contemporary Art Centre Lausanne) and Musée de l’Elysée. The following year Niblock was honored with the prestigious Foundation for Contemporary Arts John Cage Award.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261221T230000Z
SUMMARY:Phill Niblock Winter Solstice 2026: 6 Hours of Music and Film
UID:def95c55-b3b7-420a-9901-2b19aa87c03b@def9.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/phill-niblock-winter-solstice-2026/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nPercussion ensemble joins composers/soloists Brian Chase and John Hollenbeck to premiere two new works.\n\nContemporary percussion ensemble\nTalujon\npresents world premieres by\nBrian Chase\nand\nJohn Hollenbeck\n\, with both composers joining as soloists in their respective works. Each new composition blends the composers’ personal musical languages with Talujon’s disctinct voice and style.\nBrian Chase\ncomposer\, soloist\nJohn Hollenbeck\ncomposer\, soloist\nTalujon\npercussion ensemble:\nIan Antonio\nCaitlin Cawley\nDavid Cossin\nMatthew Gold\nTom Kolor\nMichael Lipsey\nMatt Ward\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nTalujon\nDescribed by the New York Times as possessing an “edgy\, unflagging energy\,” Talujon has committed itself to the growth of contemporary percussion music through diverse performance\, commissioning\, educational\, and outreach activities. Talujon is composed of percussionists Ian Antonio\, Caitlin Cawley\, David Cossin\, Matthew Gold\, Tom Kolor\, Michael Lipsey\, and Matt Ward. Highlights of Talujon’s recent engagements include appearances at the Brooklyn Academy of Music’s Next Wave Festival\, Bang on a Can’s Marathon and Long Play Festivals\, Carnegie Hall\, ISSUE Project Room\, Roulette\, Miller Theatre\, and New York Historical Society. International performances include Taipei’s Lantern Festival and Italy’s Sound Res Festival. Talujon partners with New York City Public Schools and the Midori and Friends organization to produce educational programming across New York City’s five boroughs. The ensemble’s playing can be heard on the Cantaloupe\, Tzadik\, Unseen Worlds\, Another Timbre\, New World\, Bridge\, Albany\, and Capstone record labels.\nBrooklyn-based musician\nBrian Chase\nis the drummer for Grammy-nominated rock band Yeah Yeah Yeahs\, NYC’s experimental music community and Drums and Drones\, a solo project with a compositional focus on the harmonic resonances of drums and percussion. As an exceptionally versatile performer\, Brian’s music has taken him across the world to such notable stages as the Sydney Opera House\, Metropolitan Museum of Art and Coachella as well as countless independent art spaces of music’s ‘underground’ communities. In 2018\, Chase started an independent record label\, Chaikin Records\, which spotlights pioneering figures of the avant-garde. The inaugural release on Chaikin Records was Drums and Drones: Decade\, a triple album with 144 page book\, described in The Wire as “an indispensable statement on how drummers hear sound.” Additional releases by Chase on Chaikin Records include duo albums with harpist Zeena Parkins\, pianist Anthony Coleman\, saxophonist Catherine Sikora and a collaboration with visual artist Keti Katveli. As an educator Chase has taught at Bennington College and was a guest artist at So Percussion’s SoSI festival and Yarn/Wire’s annual summer institute. Chase’s writings have appeared in John Zorn’s Arcana series\, culture blog Talkhouse and drumming trade magazine Modern Drummer.\nJohn Hollenbeck\nis a composer\, drummer\, and percussionist whose work spans jazz\, contemporary classical music\, chamber music\, and large ensemble composition. Since emerging as a bandleader in 2001\, he has led a wide range of acclaimed projects\, including the Claudia Quintet and the John Hollenbeck Large Ensemble\, both recognized for their innovative approaches to ensemble music. Hollenbeck has composed extensively for jazz ensembles\, choirs\, wind ensembles\, chamber groups\, and orchestras\, receiving commissions from major festivals\, universities\, and arts organizations in North America and Europe. His recordings and compositions have earned six GRAMMY nominations\, including nominations for the John Hollenbeck Large Ensemble and his large-ensemble arrangements. A graduate of the Eastman School of Music\, Hollenbeck studied percussion and jazz composition and was mentored by composer and arranger Bob Brookmeyer and composer Meredith Monk. His honors include a Doris Duke Performing Artist Award\, the ASCAP Jazz Vanguard Award\, and a Guggenheim Fellowship. He taught at the Jazz Institute Berlin from 2005–2016 and later joined the faculty of McGill University’s Schulich School of Music. Hollenbeck continues to perform\, compose\, and collaborate internationally as one of today’s leading creative musicians.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261124T010000Z
SUMMARY:Talujon with Brian Chase and John Hollenbeck
UID:3008e0bd-41ee-48b8-a5d8-414b5e85b64c@3008.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/talujon-brian-chase-john-hollenbeck/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $30\nPurchase Tickets\nMarc Ribot Quartet celebrates the forthcoming release of their new record\, Hurry Red Telephone.\n\nMarc Ribot Quartet\ncelebrates the release of their new record\,\nHurry Red Telephone\n\, out October 30\, 2026\, on Pi Recordings.\nMarc Ribot\nguitar\nChad Taylor\ndrums\nSebastian Steinberg\nbass\nBriggan Krauss\nalto sax & guitar\nFor more than four decades\, guitarist\nMarc Ribot\nhas made a career out of refusing to stay in one place\, moving restlessly between jazz\, rock\, No Wave\, singer/songwriter\, solo guitar\, music for film\, Cuban music\, and free improvisation\, all while leaving an unmistakable imprint on everything he touches. With\nHurry Red Telephone\n\, Ribot returns with his first all-instrumental album since 2014’s highly-lauded\nLive at the Village Vanguard\n\, which featured drummer\nChad Taylor\nand the late bassist Henry Grimes. It reunites him with Taylor in an eponymously-named band recorded over two separate sessions: one with tenor saxophonists\nJames Brandon Lewis\nand\nBrian Settles\nwith bassists\nHilliard Greene\n\; and another with alto saxophonist\nBriggan Krauss\nand bassist\nSebastian Steinberg\n(a veteran of Shrek and other Ribot experiments of the early 1990’s). Featuring four original compositions by Ribot alongside two by Haitian guitarist and composer\nFrantz Casséus\n– Ribot’s guitar teacher and an enduring musical influence – the performances reveal all of Ribot’s best qualities\, by turns volatile\, lyrical\, abrasive\, humorous\, and soulful.\nThe band takes its name from a verse from the poem “\nSeveral Tremendous\n” by\nRichard Siken\,\nwhich depicts the poet’s efforts to rebuild his ability to write and speak after a stroke robbed him of both. It’s an apt metaphor for Ribot’s pursuit of a language reconstructed from the ground up. The quartet format gives Ribot and his cohorts the freedom to do just that\, continuing the improvisational conversation Ribot and Chad Taylor had begun\, while at the same time paying homage to the guitarist’s No Wave and free jazz background. Bass and drums provide propulsion and resistance while Lewis\, Settles\, and Krauss – some of the most remarkable saxophonists on the scene – add their highly individual voices to the exchange. The music is composed\, but never contained\; its structures are launchpads for improvisation. The result is a visceral\, rollicking\, and celebratory tour-de-force.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261020T000000Z
SUMMARY:Marc Ribot Quartet: Hurry Red Telephone (Album Release)
UID:30fce0f9-e1d2-441a-b882-fbbcacf9d5a9@30fc.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/marc-ribot-quartet-hurry-red-telephone/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $55\nPurchase Tickets\nPulitzer-winning composer\, saxophonist & flutist returns with Zooid\, performing music from new album Cut You Where You Was (Pi Recordings).\n\nPulitzer-winning composer\, saxophonist\, and flutist Henry Threadgill returns with his quintet\, Zooid\, to perform music from their new album\,\nCut You Where You Was\n\, recently released on Pi Recordings.\nThe performance marks the 10th anniversary of Threadgill’s 2016 Pulitzer Prize for\nIn for a Penny\, In for a Pound\n— an album composed for Zooid which premiered at Roulette in 2014.\nHenry Threadgill\nflute\, bass flute\, and alto saxophone\nChristopher Hoffman\ncello\nLiberty Ellman\nacoustic guitar\nJose Davila\ntrombone and tuba\nElliot Kavee\ndrums\n“Sweet-and-sour\, jazz-tango-Middle-Eastern-funk [from] one of our great composers.”\n–The New York Times\n“One of jazz’s most admired innovators since the ’70s and a pillar of Chicago’s Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians\, multi-instrumentalist Henry Threadgill takes the stage with his slinky\, utterly distinctive\, and disarmingly engaging acoustic sextet\, Zooid.” –The New Yorker\nThis performance will not be recorded.\nOnly three jazz artists have won a Pulitzer Prize. In spring 2016\,\nHenry Threadgill\njoined Ornette Coleman and Wynton Marsalis as Pulitzer laureates\, when he was honored for\nIn For A Penny\, In For A Pound\n(Pi\, 2015)\, the latest album by\nZooid\n\, his unconventional quintet (reeds\, acoustic guitar\, cello\, tuba\, drums).\n“Unconventional” describes not just Henry Threadgill’s music\, but his life.\nBorn in Chicago in 1944\, Henry grew up on the South Side\, where parade bands and the blues filled the air. He played percussion\, then clarinet in the Englewood High School band\, but switched to sax at 16. Idolizing Sonny Rollins\, John Coltrane\, Ornette Coleman\, and Lester Young\, he adored Fritz Reiner’s Chicago Symphony and avant-classical composers like Luciano Berio. He was 17 when he joined the\nMuhal Richard Abrams\n’ Experimental Band\, which later expanded into the\nAssociation for the Advancement of Creative Musicians\n(AACM)\; there he found like-minded musical explorers.\nIt was in the East Village—long a seedy\, tumultuous haven for outsiders of all types—that Henry Threadgill launched the unconventional concepts that led to his Pulitzer-winning art.\nAIR\n(Artists In Residence)\, his 1970s trio\, reimagined ragtime without the piano—a lot like dropping the electric guitar from rock. His 1980s\nSextett\n\, pairing complex compositions and dynamic soloists\, combined heft and agility\, and birthed the “little big band” sound. In the 1990s\,\nVery Very Circus\nstepped deeper into unorthodoxy\, with two electric guitars\, two tubas\, a trombone/French horn\, drums\, Henry’s alto sax and flute\, and frequent add-ons. With\nMake A Move\n\, a fluid lineup mixing French horn\, tubas\, electric and acoustic guitars\, and cello\, he began exploring the approaches to composing and improvising that led to Zooid. From 2000 on\, Zooid became his primary vehicle.\nAs a composer and improviser\, Henry sees artistic process and product as inseparable\, the essence of jazz. Duke Ellington and Charles Mingus strove toward the same goal. Rooted in that history\, Henry’s solutions have taken radical new tacks. For Zooid\, the Pulitzer committee explained\, “A set of three note intervals assigned to each player…serves as the starting point for improvisation.” Zooid’s musicians make in-the-moment decisions about structure\, shaping the work-in-process. The unpredictable results are jazz’s “sound of surprise” updated for the 21st century.\nAfter decades of probing music\, cult status\, and critical acclaim\, Threadgill’s Pulitzer Prize caps growing high-culture recognition: 2016 Doris Duke Artist Award\; 2015 Doris Duke Impact Award\; 2008 United States Artist Fellowship\; 2003 Guggenheim Fellowship. He is especially proud of being the first black non-classical musician to get a Copland House Residency Award. In July 2016\, the annual Leadership Conference of the Vietnam Veterans of America honored him with their Excellence in the Arts award—a very special moment for the only Vietnam veteran ever awarded a Pulitzer for music.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261123T010000Z
SUMMARY:Henry Threadgill’s Zooid
UID:fd190e82-5c03-4042-9cc4-9bc783a6c991@fd19.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/henry-threadgill-zooid-cut-you-where-you-was/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nJazz Generations Initiative Continuum Fellow presents a three night residency featuring poets\, musicians\, and artists across generations.\n\n“Poet Jayne Cortez once spoke the words\, ‘Find your own voice and use it\, use your own voice and find it.’ There is a through-line that connects poetry\, music and visual art … Both are comprised of units of sound\, all have a relationship with rhythm … Tonight we attempt to merge these worlds while still respecting the nuance of each … We are all human\, however each with a signature uniquely our own.” — James Brandon Lewis\nAward-winning saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\n\, a\nJazz Generations Initiative (JGI)\nContinuum Fellow\n\, presents\nThru Line\n\, a three-night residency at Roulette as part of his 2026 fellowship. The series brings together improvisers\, poets\, musicians\, and multidisciplinary artists from across media and generations.\nThis evening’s performance features a duo set by\nJames Brandon Lewis\nand poet\nAnne Waldman\n\, along with duo performances by\nOliver Lake\nand\nJosh Werner\n\,\nTuesday Taylor\nand\nMelanie Dyer\,\nand\nTaylor Pace\nand\nKokayi\n.\n“A Saxophonist Who Embodies and Transcends Tradition” — The NY Times\n“James Brandon Lewis does not take the easy road. Having forged a singular sound on the tenor saxophone\, he could simply devise settings that showcase his brawny tone. Instead\, he has rooted his recent music in extramusical research.” — DownBeat\nPresented by the Jazz Generations Initiative in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nThe JGI Continuum Fellowship\nconvenes a cohort of New York-based early-to mid-career musicians and multidisciplinary artists to curate concerts and programs that foster creative exchange across artistic disciplines and generations.\nTenor saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\nhas emerged as a leading figure on the improvised music scene in New York City and across the world. The winner of seven DownBeat magazine awards (including “artist of the year” in the 2025 critics’ poll)\, and a 2026 Guggenheim Fellowship\, Lewis has led ensembles on some of the most prominent stages across the world\, from the Village Vanguard to the Bimhuis Muziekgebouw. He has received accolades from The New York Times\, NPR Music and countless other outlets. The late Sonny Rollins once told Lewis\, “When I listen to you\, I listen to Buddha\, I listen to Confucius… I listen to the deeper meaning of life. You are keeping the world in balance.” Lewis relocated to New York City in 2012\, and since then he has become known as one of the leading figures on the scene\, uniting his background in gospel and jazz with an interest in Western classical music\, hip-hop\, punk rock\, other music traditions from around the globe\, and art forms outside of music. His celebrated projects include the James Brandon Lewis Trio\, the American Book Award-winning Heroes Are Gang Leaders\, the Red Lily Quintet\, and his ongoing collaboration with the Messthetics\, which has yielded two critically acclaimed albums on Impulse! Records. Lewis\, who recently earned a PhD in Creativity from Rowan University in New Jersey\, also works across mediums as a writer and poet.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261010T000000Z
SUMMARY:Find Your Own Voice: James Brandon Lewis and Anne Waldman // Oliver Lake and Josh Werner // Tuesday Taylor and Melanie Dyer // Taylor Pace and Kokayi
UID:32e4c9d6-7ad3-40b0-aac3-7c62a04dbe55@32e4.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/james-brandon-lewis-taylor-pace-tuesday-taylor/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $15\nPurchase Tickets\nAn interactive workshop exploring the ideas behind the artist's most recent collaboration with Rafael Osés\, X Amendments.\n\nComposer/improviser/writer Carla Kihlstedt and poet/artist Rafael Osés lead an interactive workshop exploring the process behind their latest collaboration\,\nX AMENDMENTS\n. We’ll redact\, amend and reorder the text of the Bill of Rights to create miniature improvised scores out of the rubble of it all with members of the\nX AMENDMENTS\nband. All experience levels welcome!\nX AMENDMENTS\nis an improvised sonic structure for an orchestra of voices\, electric guitars\, and an audience that uses the Bill of Rights as its source material. The performance is an experiment in individual and collective freedoms and an exploration of rights\, responsibilities\, agency\, and restrictions.\nA poem from X AMENDMENTS by Rafael Osés:\n*FREE FOR ALL ROULETTE MEMBERS *\nA livestream will be available and archived for future viewing.\nCarla Kihlstedt\nis an American composer\, improviser\, writer and educator who plays violin/viola and sings\, often simultaneously. She loves the economy\, intimacy\, and unapologetic directness of song. In her larger pieces (\nBlack Inscription\, Necessary Monsters\, 26 Little Deaths\, At Night We Walk in Circles and Are Consumed by Fire\,\nPandæmonium\, X Amendments\, 6 Dioramas) she explores the complexity of a single idea through many different lenses.\nCarla is a founding member of the experimental metal band Sleepytime Gorilla Museum\, the composers collective Tin Hat (formerly Tin Hat Trio)\, the song-based projects Rabbit Rabbit Radio and 2 Foot Yard\, and the improvised bands Causing A Tiger\, and Minamo. She is on the faculty of the Contemporary Musical Arts Department of the New England Conservatory\, the MFA in Music Composition program at the Vermont College of Fine Arts at CalArts\, and she co-facilitated the Creative Gesture Lab for composers and choreographers at the Banff Centre for Arts and Creativity. She is currently incubating two new choral projects: Long for This World—an educational\, environmentally-centered youth chorus project\; Feral Choir—an experimental vocal group rooted in the physicality of singing bodies.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261006T230000Z
SUMMARY:Carla Kihlstedt & Rafael Osés: A Workshop on the Process Behind X AMENDMENTS
UID:fa744856-baaf-47b9-8409-341febb10d9c@fa74.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/workshop-carla-kihlstedt/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $42.23\nPurchase Tickets\nTerre Thaemlitz presents a debut live performance on piano\, followed by a conversation with Ciarán Finlayson\, as part of DURATIONS Festival.\n\nThe legendary house producer\, DJ\, and writer\nTerre Thaemlitz [aka DJ Sprinkles]\nwill perform her first composition for solo piano live at Roulette. The performance will be followed by a conversation with Triple Canopy contributing editor\nCiarán Finlayson\n\, who will speak with Thaemlitz about her music as well as her criticism\, which often takes up the defects of a culture industry that offers artists “exposure” instead of wages.\nStarting in 2024\, Terre Thaemlitz began a new series of durational piano solo performances. Unlike Thaemlitz’s computer-composed Rubato Series digital piano concerts of the past\, these current performances are acoustic and played on a grand piano (180 cm or larger). Each show consists of a new composition using improvisational systems\, with occasional prepared-piano techniques. While sonically more dynamic than Thaemlitz’s notorious 32-hour-long acoustic piano piece\,\nMeditation on Wage Labor and the Death of the Album\n(2012)\, this new series continues the minimalist\, ambient\, and neo-expressionist piano playing developed over the past three decades in Thaemlitz’s various electroacoustic and house albums.\nThe first performance of this series took place in June 2024\, as a close to Trajal Harrell’s five-year stint as resident choreographer at the Schauspielhaus Zürich. Both Harrell and Thaemlitz have their own histories within the US voguing ball scene\, and both have continually examined the tensions between culturally minor and major audio movements. On that night\, Harrell gave the final performance of her acclaimed group choreography for Keith Jarrett’s\nKöln Concert\n\, followed by what was lightheartedly billed as Thaemlitz’s “Zürich Concert.” Thaemlitz’s longtime collaborator Mark Fell was quick to note\, “You’re no Keith Jarrett”—the stylistic and ideological incongruities between Jarrett and Thaemlitz being the point.\nThe performances are not recorded\, streamed\, or archived. This is in keeping with Thaemlitz’s longstanding no-recording policy\, rooted in a critical rejection of today’s homogenizing archive industries. This policy began in the early 2010s\, in specific reaction to sudden federal and corporate financial interests in building LGBT archives\, which went against Thaemlitz’s own strategies of the closet\, invisibility\, secrecy\, withholding\, and remaining culturally minor. It is also a means of interfering with the audio market’s and audiences’ tendencies to fetishize recordings of Jarrett-style “you had to be there” performances. Rather\, Thaemlitz privately records the preparatory sessions leading up to each show. These will eventually be self-released through Comatonse Recordings as collections containing four to five separate takes\, giving a sense of what happened at the live performances while still refusing to settle on a single take as “the one.”\nPresented in collaboration with Triple Canopy.\nRoulette members – please email\nmembership@roulette.org\nfor more information about member discounts.\nTriple Canopy\nis committed to cultivating unconventional\, unassimilated forms of thought and expression\, and to meaningfully supporting the people who produce them. The magazine collaborates with contributors on artworks\, essays\, fictions\, conversations\, performances\, and books [among other media] from conception to realization\, navigating the digital and physical realms where ideas and audiences take shape. In doing so\, Triple Canopy strives to not only analyze but alter the structures that influence whose voices are heard\, whose stories are circulated\, and whose experiences are valued.\nFounded in 2007 and based in New York City\, Triple Canopy is a 501(c)(3) nonprofit organization\, has been certified by W.A.G.E.\, and is a member of the Coalition of Small Arts New York City (CoSA NYC).\nDURATIONS Festival\nis a week-long\, multi-disciplinary exploration of sound and connection. Now in its fifth iteration\, the festival’s programming is its most robust yet\, with seven days of music\, talks\, and audiovisual work taking place between Public Records\, Pioneer Works\, Roulette\, and Whitney Museum of American Art.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261116T010000Z
SUMMARY:DURATIONS: An Evening with Terre Thaemlitz [Live Piano] + Conversation with Ciarán Finlayson
UID:17299d6d-f947-48e7-8dc9-0d7e81409ab1@1729.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/durations-triple-canopy-terre-thaemlitz-ciaran-finlayson/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nChoreographer explores nonsense-making through fragmentation\, the compression of sound\, and movement scores for her Roulette Commission.\n\nJessica Cook shares new material developed as part of her Roulette Commission.\nThe solo\nBreaks\nby Jessica Cook is a physical exploration of nonsense-making through fragmentation\, the compression of sound\, and movement scores. The work uses improvisation scores that splice\, isolate\, reference\, and congeal macro and micro textures\, paired with constructed sound costumes and “toys” created by the artist.\nJessica Cook\nperformer & choreographer\nAdditional performers TBA\n*FREE FOR ALL ROULETTE MEMBERS *\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nJessica Cook\n(b. Kingston\, NY) has worked as a Brooklyn based choreographer for over two decades. She combines movement\, the physicality of sound making\, and sculpture in creating absurdist and strained worlds. She has presented work in venues such as La MaMa Moves Festival\, LMCC’s River to River Festival\, Pageant\, Roulette Intermedium\, Movement Research at Judson Memorial Church\, and others.\nAs a dance artist she is currently working with Tess Dworman (\nThe Con\n)\, Benjamin Akio Kimmitch (\nAllegra\n)\, visual artist Amy Beecher\, Sam Kim\, and director/dreamworker Amanda Lovejoy Street.\nShe has been an artist-in-residence at The Chocolate Factory Theater\, Movement Research\, NY Live Arts Fresh Tracks\, and MoMA PS1. She is a 2005 graduate of the SUNY Purchase Dance Conservatory and Pacific College of Health and Science.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261121T010000Z
SUMMARY:Breaks: A Solo Performance by Jessica Cook (Night 2)
UID:bda15203-3f30-4041-bce4-c797368ea945@bda1.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/jessica-cook-night-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nThree performers collide\, spin\, fall\, and intertwine in an athletic maze of child’s play and menacing acts.\n\nHow Dogs Play\nexplores the complex presence of aggression in intimacy\, excavating the seen and unseen ruptures between self and other. In a tumult of childlike play\, rivalrous antics\, and feats of maniacal athleticism\, this work tunes the dial of intensity to animate an underworld of feeling. We work\, dare\, push the edge\, cross that line\, slipping between human\, animal\, and monster. This dance is for the State Champ\, the Junkie\, the Hustler\, those who push their bodies into the unknown\, chasing the sensational and the spiritual.\nAmelia Heintzelman\nchoreography & performance\nBrendan Elefante\, Jack Dexter\nperformance\nTommy Martinez\nsound\nTess Michaelson\ndramaturgy\nSylvie Hayes-Wallace\ncostume\n“Amelia Heintzelman charged the space with a burst of energy wavering formidably between violence and vulnerability.” –Gia Kourlas\, New York Times\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nAmelia Heintzelman\nis a dancer\, choreographer\, and teacher. Her work has been supported by residencies\, commissions\, and grants through Center for Performance Research\, Draftwork at Danspace Project\, Foundation for Contemporary Arts\, Issue Project Room\, Movement Research at the Judson Church\, New York Foundation for the Arts\, Pageant\, Snug Harbor\, and UCross Foundation. Most recently\, she’s worked as a collaborating performer for Kim Brandt\, Jesi Cook\, Ayano Elson\, Deborah Hay\, Melinda Ring and Alexa West. She is on teaching faculty at Movement Research and Pageant\, and rarely/sometimes teaches Comedy Pilates.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261203T010000Z
SUMMARY:Amelia Heintzelman: How Dogs Play (Night 2)
UID:ce30b679-bd4a-4113-9e9f-fdee4de55e1a@ce30.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/amelia-heintzelman-how-dogs-play-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\nOne of the longest-standing musicians’ associations in the United States celebrates 61 years of fostering creative music.\n\nAssociation for the Advancement of Creative Musicians’s (AACM) New York City Chapter\nhosts a two-night celebration of their 61st anniversary\, with Richarda Abrams as the Mistress of Ceremonies.\nSET 1\nDavid Virelles\nsolo piano\nSET 2\nThurman Barker & Noah Barker Duo\nThurman Barker\ndrums\, percussion\, bells\nNoah Barker\npiano\n(AACM New York City Chapter Inc.’s 2025 Emerging Artist)\nSET 3\nThurman Barker\nsolo percussion\nSET 4\nThurman Barker’s new string quartet plays a world premiere of\nROCK CITY (Zion National Park)\nThe Rhythm Method\nLeah Asher\nviolin\nMarina Kifferstein\nviolin\nCarrie Frey\nviola\nMeaghan Burke\ncello\nThis evening is presented by AACM New York City Chapter\, Inc. in Partnership with Roulette.\nThe Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians (AACM)\n\, a non-profit tax-exempt arts organization co-founded in 1965 by Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams\, Jodie Christian\, Kelan Phil Cohran\, and Steve McCall\, is one of the oldest musicians associations in the United States. Since its founding in Chicago\, Illinois in 1965\, the organization’s members have developed a body of work that encompasses the spectrum of traditional and contemporary music.\nThe Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians New York City Chapter\, Inc. a non-profit tax-exempt entity was established in 1983 by AACM members who moved to New York. The AACM is significantly unique in that it has nurtured and brought to fruition so many accomplished composers and performers. The organization’s artists have developed a body of work that has achieved lasting significance across borders of music genres and geography to play a critical role in the ever evolving process of music creation and its vital influence throughout the world. AACM members who have been featured in our programs include: Anthony Braxton\, Roscoe Mitchell\, Wadada Leo Smith\, Henry Threadgill\, Thurman Barker\, Adegoke Steve Colson\, Iqua Colson\, Chico Freeman\, Amina Claudine Myers\, George Lewis\, Reggie Nicholson\, Peggy Abrams\, Richarda Abrams\, Laroy Wallace McMillan and posthumously Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams\, Leonard E. Jones\, Frank Gordon\, Joseph Jarman\, Leroy Jenkins\, Kalaparusha Ahra Difda\, Lester Bowie\, Steve McCall\, Fred Anderson\, John Stubblefield and Fred Hopkins. AACM New York City Chapter\, Inc. continues to produce concerts annually in New York. Our members have received degrees from top Universities and have expertise in many areas ie. artists\, painters\, and novelists. Because of the AACM New York City Chapter\, Inc. our members have gained international recognition as accomplished musicians and composers\, many of whom have been invited by renowned orchestras throughout the USA and Europe to perform their music. Our members are master musician composers and have taught in major universities all over the world.\nDavid Virelles\nis a pianist and composer who grew up in a musical family in Santiago de Cuba\, immersed in both the Afro – Caribbean diaspora’s music and Western classical traditions. While he considers his work “a hundred percent traditional\,” drawing from a synthesis of traditions\, he creates a unique\, syncretic personal music rather than merely mixing elements or recreating existing styles. This vision was showcased in Virelles’ world premiere of his interdisciplinary piece ORO\, commissioned by Carnegie Hall\, and at the premiere of Vaivén\, commissioned by the Pierre Boulez Saal. In addition to his solo work\, Virelles has performed/ collaborated with Henry Threadgill\, Ravi Coltrane\, Andrew Cyrille\, Reggie Workman\, Tomasz Stańko\, Bill Frisell\, Román Díaz\, Milford Graves\, Terri Lyne Carrington\, Marcus Gilmore\, Mark Turner\, Chris Potter\, Tom Harrell\, Paul Motian\, José Luis Quintana “Changuito”\, Stanley Cowell\, Hermeto Pascoal\, Chucho Valdés\, and Juan Pablo Torres. Virelles’ 2012 album Continuum appeared on many “Best of the Year” lists\, being selected as the #1 album by The New York Times.\nThurman Barker\nis a celebrated percussionist who built his career on innovation. As a professional musician\, composer and college professor\, he brings endless passion and decades of experience together to entertain\, educate\, and expand the horizons of his art. His career has spanned decades\, and his talent encompasses and crosses over into many musical genres. From jazz and blues to contemporary\, classical and beyond\, his sound blurs traditionally rigid lines to the delight of his audiences. Barker’s wealth of knowledge was built through countless incredible collaborators. Most notable musical experiences have been with the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians. A charter member of the group\, Thurman first appeared in AACM productions with Joseph Jarman’s pioneering ensembles. He then went on to record and play with many members\, including Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams\, Amina Claudine Meyers\, Anthony Braxton\, Leroy Jenkins\, Roscoe Mitchell and Henry Threadgill. After moving to New York in the late 70s\, Barker worked and recorded with Sam Rivers and Cecil Taylor. Since creating UPTEE RECORDS in the early 80s\, Barker has released eight recordings as a leader. In 2016\, Barker began writing for Chamber Orchestra. His first\, entitled\nSouth Side Suite\,\npremiered at Bard College in the fall of 2017. S.S.S. had its New York premiere at Roulette 2019. His second score PANDEMIC FEVER had its premiere at DePaul University in Chicago October 1\,2021. His recent score entitled MR. SPEED-STR will have its premiere Fall 2022. Thurman Barker is a recipient of a 2022 NYSCA award for composition.\nNoah Barker\nis a contemporary pianist whose work spans two decades of performing\, recording\, and scoring. His professional work includes scoring the documentary Beekeeper\, which aired as part of PBS’s Films BYkids series. Barker has released three albums of original music: Meditations (2025) on Tableaux Records\, and Subtle Frequencies (2024) and Instrumental Memory (2022) on FPE Records. He has also self-released a number of projects as a solo artist and through his experimental project Noisebody. Barker holds a master’s degree in jazz piano performance from SUNY Purchase and serves on the faculty at Rhodes School of Music. He is currently based in Los Angeles\, where he continues to perform and produce music while writing a book of piano etudes.\nThe Rhythm Method\nis praised as “fierce\, fearless\, and virtuosic… unapologetically stylistically omnivorous and versatile” (New Music Box) and “trailblazing…skillful composer-performers” (The New Yorker)\, The Rhythm Method strives to reimagine the string quartet in a contemporary\, feminist context. The four performer-composers of The Rhythm Method continually expand their sonic and expressive palette through the use of improvisation\, vocalization\, graphic notation\, songwriting\, and theater. The Rhythm Method has given performances at Roulette\, Joe’s Pub\, The Stone\, the Met Museum\, the Americas Society\, and the Noguchi Museum\, and has been featured at the Lucerne Festival Forward\, MATA Festival\, the Ukrainian Contemporary Music Festival\, American Composers Orchestra’s Sonic Festival\, Music Mondays\, and TriBeCa New Music\, among others. The quartet tours regularly both in the US and abroad\, and has performed internationally in France\, Austria\, and Switzerland. The Rhythm Method’s ongoing activities include the Hidden Mothers Project\, a programming initiative that highlights works by historical women composers\, and Broad Statements\, an annual mini-festival celebrating creative music-making by women\, nonbinary\, and gender-expansive people in a wide array of artistic styles.\nRicharda Abrams\nrecorded\nSong for All\n(Black Saint Records) with her father Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams and performed in Dr. Muhal Richard Abrams’ Ensemble at Roulette Intermedium. She is a member of Amina Claudine Myers’ Generation IV\, a gospel group\, Myers’ Voice Choir\, and has toured worldwide. She is a 4X AUDELCO award-winning actress/singer/playwright/producer of\nFirst By Faith: The Life of Mary McLeod Bethune\n\,\nBethune: Our Black Velvet Rose and Big Mama and Me\n. Theatre productions include:\nHaving Our Say: The Delany Sisters First 100 Years\n(East Lynne Theater Co.)\,\nTelling Tales Out of School\n(New Federal Theater dir.Woodie King Jr.)\,\nAmerican Rot\nand\nLast Days of Judas Iscariot\n(La Mama\, dir. Estelle Parsons)\,\nFear Itself\n(Crossroads)\,and\nGuess Who’s Coming to Dinner (\nIvoryton Playhouse). Film/TV:\nThe Subject\n\,\nThe Riverside Bench\n\,\nOreos With Attitude\n(Showtime)\, and\nSesame Street\n.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261219T010000Z
SUMMARY:AACM NYC: Celebrating 61 Years of Creative Music with David Virelles\, Thurman Barker\, Noah Barker\, and The Rhythm Method
UID:daedf60f-416f-417d-bd51-d6fa7488d29f@daed.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/aacm-celebrating-61-years-of-creative-music-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nA celebration of the groundbreaking work of video artist Kit Fitzgerald\, showcasing her lush visual style in two U.S. premieres.\n\nRoulette presents the work of video artist\nKit Fitzgerald\n\, showcasing her lush visual style in two US premieres:\nVenezia: Dream of Consciousness\n\, a new-narrative video made with composer\nPeter Gordon\n\, featuring the noted actor\nTomas Arana\non a nocturnal search through the back streets of Venice\; and\nOutside the Box (Yellow)\n\, a performance that explores the parameters of beauty and abstraction through a real-time mix of video drawing\, image processing\, and sensuality. Featured musicians in\nOutside the Box (Yellow)\ninclude Peter Gordon\, Max Gordon\, and Blake Leyh.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nKit Fitzgerald\nhas created groundbreaking works of video art and live video performance. She collaborated with musicians\, choreographers\, and theater and opera directors\, and also directed music videos and documentaries. Fitzgerald’s work is in the collection of The Museum of Modern Art and was twice included in the Whitney Biennial. She received grants and commissions from The Rockefeller Foundation\, the National Endowment for the Arts\, Tokyo Broadcasting System\, NHK\, and Sony Japan. Festival prizes included firsts at the International Electronic Cinema Festival (Montreux)\, Tokyo International HDTV Festival\, and International Women’s Biennale (Vienna). Her work is distributed by Electronic Arts Intermix\, New York.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261206T010000Z
SUMMARY:Kit Fitzgerald
UID:f6ffd4be-ed8e-469a-9808-cb6d54efb131@f6ff.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/kit-fitzgerald/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Triple Canopy’s annual symposium is a manifestation of the magazine’s concerns and collaborations\, in person and in real time. The multiday gathering brings together artists\, writers\, performers\, and researchers to address the role of culture in responding (or contributing) to a range of critical issues.\nFree w/ RSVP\n1PM\nSpeaking Without Tongues\nCooper Jacoby\, Christopher Kulendran Thomas & Maya Man\nGenerated audio has permeated the sonic landscape\, whether as deepfaked soundbites or stilted TikTok voiceovers\, and is increasingly difficult to distinguish from human speech. The artists Cooper Jacoby\, Maya Man\, and Christopher Kulendran Thomas will discuss how they have each trained artificial voices and deployed them to explore loss and desire\, power and performance\, labor\, and celebrity.\n3PM\nTechnomisoginary\nSophie Lewis & Kalindi Vora\nThe cultural theorists Sophie Lewis and Kalindi Vora will discuss the ways in which misogyny and colonial ideology shape contemporary technologies\, as well as how we might combat tech leaders’ annihilatory ethos. Together\, they will consider a vision of liberated reproduction (both social and biological) and how devalued\, feminized forms of labor might be reimagined and properly remunerated.\n5PM\nBets in a Burning House\nDavid Hill\, Ayesha A. Siddiqi & Jacob Silverman\nDrawing from their writing and research\, David Hill\, Ayesha A. Siddiqi\, and Jacob Silverman will address the rise of prediction markets\, as well as their historical precedents and contemporary policies and politics. As prediction markets push to “financialize everything\,” who will profit from speculating on—or manipulating—the future\, and at what cost?\n8PM\nGhosted\, Too\nMorgan Bassichis\n“Adoration has a way of keeping the deceased at least a little bit alive\,” Bassichis writes. “I will sometimes refer to them as ghosts\, but a bit reluctantly\, because that is a term I have only ever heard from the living.” Considering ghosts as manifestations of “unfinished business\,” Bassichis will offer provisional reflections on performance as a mode of necromancy\, or “visitationship.”\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261115T010000Z
SUMMARY:Triple Canopy Symposium 2026 (Day 2)
UID:f1366355-37b0-4bb5-bdb5-70c18ff457cf@f136.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/triple-canopy-symposium-2026-day-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Seeing in the Dark\nis an evening of new music that offers different ways to light our path through darkness — these three transformative works seek to provide us new ways to see:\nBrendon Randall-Myers\nand\nAnnie Blythe\npresent the world premiere arrangement of\nOnly in the Dark\,\na gorgeous cello concerto for the chamber orchestral forces of\nContemporaneous\nand\nAnnie Blythe\nas the soloist. The piece is inspired by Randall Myers’ and Blythe’s neurodivergent experiences and transforms our expectations as we connect through the senses to our world and ourselves.\nLila Meretzky’s\nextraordinary work\nto crave and to have\nilluminates “the gulf between what is and what ought to be.”\nThe world premiere of the next book in\nPaul Pinto’s\nyears-in-the-making meta-opera\nEstabanico\n\, featuring members of\nMantra Percussion\nand\nRecap Percussion\n\, follows a Moorish conquistador as it pulls apart threads of the stories of the past. Over a sprawling span of time\, the work offers an expansive new vision of the history of the Americas.\nContemporaneous\nis an ensemble of 25 musicians whose mission is to bring to life the most transformative music by living composers through performances\, commissions\, recordings\, and educational programs. Described as “exact and detailed\, but also lively and openly dancing” (The New York Times) and “leading new music towards its better self” (I Care If You Listen)\, Contemporaneous particularly champions the creation of large-scale works and “dream projects\,” which composers might not otherwise have opportunities to realize due to scale.\nBased in New York City and active throughout the United States\, Contemporaneous has premiered over 200 new works\, and has been presented by such institutions as Carnegie Hall\, Lincoln Center\, Park Avenue Armory\, the Los Angeles Philharmonic\, Walt Disney Concert Hall\, PROTOTYPE Festival\, Merkin Concert Hall\, MATA Festival\, St. Ann’s Warehouse\, and Bang on a Can and has worked with such artists as David Byrne\, Donnacha Dennehy\, Iarla Ó Lionáird\, Dawn Upshaw\, and Julia Wolfe. Contemporaneous’ programming has also received acclaim from community members\, artists\, and press of all kinds\, with the ensemble’s recent performance of Stranger Love being listed as one of 2023’s “best classical music performances” by the New York Times.\nContemporaneous also leads participatory programs for public school students in the communities where the ensemble performs. These workshops are designed to instill a passion for new music and to convey the power of careful listening and meaningful expression through music. The ensemble has held residencies at such institutions as Colgate University\, Hamilton College\, City University of New York\, the University of New Orleans\, Williams College\, and Bard College\, where the group was founded in 2010. Contemporaneous is a federally tax-exempt not-for-profit organization\, supported by a dedicated community of listeners.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261021T000000Z
SUMMARY:Contemporaneous\, Recap\, and Mantra present: Seeing in the Dark
UID:e9621e8b-3e60-402f-b75a-84db80079ec8@e962.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/contemporaneous-mantra-percussion-recap-percussion-seeing-in-the-dark/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Mutual Mentorship for Musicians (M³\n)\, a visionary and non-hierarchical mentorship initiative created by musicians\nJen Shyu\nand\nSara Serpa\n\, presents its\n5\nth annual in-person festival featuring some of the most groundbreaking composer-performers in the jazz\, creative\, electronic\, singer-songwriter\, experimental\, and traditional music scenes\, and beyond.\nM³ Festival 2026 Lineup:\nTerri Lyne Carrington • William Parker •  Patricia Nicholson • Theo Bleckmann • Martha Redbone • Marta Sanchez • Caroline Davis • Aaron Whitby • Alexandria DeWalt • Allan Mednard • Carmen Quill • Carolina Borja-Marroquin • Chanelle Ignant •  Coco Elysses • Devon Gates • Elé Howell • Ge Linna • Kweku Aggrey  • Mama Dee • Marcus Grant • Melissa Almaguer • Miranda Agnew • Morgan Guerin • Noa Fort • Paige Brown • Rashaan Carter • Stephan Crump • Summer Kodama • Tomas Fujiwara • Unma Allinar • Vitor Gonçalves • Yuhan Su\nView the latest lineup\nHERE\n.\nRoulette members receive a discount – please email membership@roulette.org for more information.\nPresented by\nM³\nin partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 7pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nMutual Mentorship for Musicians (M³)\nis a non-profit\, 501(c)3 organization and platform created to empower\, elevate\, normalize and give visibility to musicians of historically underrepresented gender identities (including cis women\, trans women\, trans men and non-binary) in intersection with race\, sexuality\, or ability across generations in the US and worldwide\, through a radical model of mentorship and musical collaborative commissions.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261108T000000Z
SUMMARY:M3 Festival 2026: Terri Lyne Carrington\, William Parker\, Theo Bleckmann\, Martha Redbone\, and More
UID:170b6e34-6f20-4a1c-a22a-48a30b04fd3d@170b.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/m3-festival-2026/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nA duet of video game visuals\, modular synth\, guitar\, and voice exploring the psycho-sexual inner world of a girl-child in the '80s.\n\nArtist/musician\nErika M. Anderson (EMA)\nand visual artist\nTabitha Nikolai\npremiere\nMemory As A Rock Out Of Reach\,\nan hour long duet performance that uses video game technologies to build out the psycho-sexual inner world of a girl-child in the ’80s. Soundtracked by modular synth and guitar\, a narrator searches for her shape amid commercial detritus\, degraded 3D scans of suburbia\, and a Polly Pocket apocalypse.\nErika Anderson\nmusic\, sound\, narration\nTabitha Nikolai\nlive visuals\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nErika M. Anderson (EMA)\nand\nTabitha Nikolai\ncreate live digital experiences that transcend genre. With a mixed background of DIY music\, art\, and hacked together immersive technology\, the duo investigate the potential for visceral\, emotive storytelling using tools often reserved for slick AAA sedation. Their individual work has been featured in places like MoMA PS1\, the Barbican Centre\, and the Journal of Gaming and Virtual Worlds.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261031T000000Z
SUMMARY:Memory as a Rock Out of Reach: EMA and Tabitha Nikolai
UID:57a429f3-035d-4147-a866-57a382c28fdf@57a4.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/erika-m-anderson-ema-tabitha-nikolai/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nChoreographer explores nonsense-making through fragmentation\, the compression of sound\, and movement scores for her Roulette Commission.\n\nJessica Cook shares new material developed as part of her Roulette Commission.\nThe solo\nBreaks\nby Jessica Cook is a physical exploration of nonsense-making through fragmentation\, the compression of sound\, and movement scores. The work uses improvisation scores that splice\, isolate\, reference\, and congeal macro and micro textures\, paired with constructed sound costumes and “toys” created by the artist.\nJessica Cook\nperformer & choreographer\nAdditional performers TBA\n*FREE FOR ALL ROULETTE MEMBERS *\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nJessica Cook\n(b. Kingston\, NY) has worked as a Brooklyn based choreographer for over two decades. She combines movement\, the physicality of sound making\, and sculpture in creating absurdist and strained worlds. She has presented work in venues such as La MaMa Moves Festival\, LMCC’s River to River Festival\, Pageant\, Roulette Intermedium\, Movement Research at Judson Memorial Church\, and others.\nAs a dance artist she is currently working with Tess Dworman (\nThe Con\n)\, Benjamin Akio Kimmitch (\nAllegra\n)\, visual artist Amy Beecher\, Sam Kim\, and director/dreamworker Amanda Lovejoy Street.\nShe has been an artist-in-residence at The Chocolate Factory Theater\, Movement Research\, NY Live Arts Fresh Tracks\, and MoMA PS1. She is a 2005 graduate of the SUNY Purchase Dance Conservatory and Pacific College of Health and Science.
DURATION:PT2H
