BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nContemporary East presents an evening of improvisational music rooted in Eastern aesthetics.\n\nContemporary East\npresents an evening of new improvisational music incorporating Eastern aesthetics and techniques. These innovative artists are developing a tradition that treasures the measure and quality of the breath\, honors vacancy and silence\, and explores alternate approaches to the art of improvisation.\nThe evening will include\n:\nA collaboration between\nIkue Mori\n(electronics)\,\nCharmaine Lee\n(voice and electronics)\, and\nNava Dunkelman\n(percussion).\nA performance of\nPercussion Peace\n\, a solo by\nReggie Nicholson\n(percussion)\n. Nicholson will also perform\ntwo short improvised collaborations with\nYuko Fujiyama\n(piano) and\nNava Dunkelman\n(percussion).\nPresented by Contemporary East in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nFounded in 2022 by Yuko Fujiyama\, Contemporary East highlights the beauty and depth of Eastern expressions in improvisational music. We seek to cultivate meaningful opportunities for artists of diverse backgrounds and disciplines to collaborate and share their work through public programs\, while building awareness of aesthetics in Asian-inspired music.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261015T000000Z
SUMMARY:Contemporary East:  Ikue Mori and Reggie Nicholson
UID:21c0ff84-d4b7-4f51-a3d1-2f8c8c774eee@21c0.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/contemporary-east-ikue-mori-charmaine-lee-nava-dunkelman-reggie-nicholson/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nBaird and Williams celebrate their first duo album on Outside Time Records\, opened by an improvised cello and bass duo.\n\nKa Baird\nand\nChris Ryan Williams\ncelebrate the release of\nEnthalpy\n\, their first duo album on Outside Time Records (out Sept. 25\, 2026).\nReflecting on the album\, Jonathan Williger writes:\n“The music flies out in bursts\; it seeps and oozes\, flows with intention. It is an incredibly rhythmic work without any hint of meter\, full of sounds that punctuate with the thud of a dart hitting a bullseye. Williams’ capability to conjure swirling atmospherics\, as well as his remarkable ability to pull fragments of melody from the haze\, fit puzzle-like into Baird’s exclamations and the eruption of electronics that often dovetail them.”\nKa Baird\nflutes\, voice\, electronics\nChris Ryan Williams\ntrumpet\, electronics\n“Ka Baird’s music occupies the space where\, by\nbreathing\, the everyday meets the sublime.\n” –\nResident Advisor\n“What kind of thing—what type of music\, what\ncategory of experience—is Chris Williams’ Odu:\nVibration II?…. Descending into its hidden depths\, you\nmight decide that it’s an exercise in deep listening or\nan explosion of linear timekeeping.\n”\n– Pitchfork\nCellist/composer\nLeila Bordreuil\nand bassist/composer\nLuke Stewart\nplay a duo set exploring feedback\, improvisation\, and the expanded sonic possibilities of their instruments.\nLeila Bordreuil\ncello\nLuke Stewart\ndouble bass\n“Bordreuil creates steadily scathing music\, favoring long and corrosive atonalities.”—New York Times\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nChris Ryan Williams\nis an artist and musician based in Brooklyn\, NY whose work unfolds through electroacoustic composition and performance installation. His EP\nLiv\ne earned praise from Jazz Right Now and The Quietus for its “dazzling collaged pieces that ricochet between improvised passages and written material” (Peter Margasak\, The Quietus). His debut LP\nOdu: Vibration II\n\, praised by Pitchfork and named one of NPR’s Best New Albums\, reimagines a journey through Plato’s cave.\nKa Baird\nis a performer\, sound artist\, musician and composer based in New York City.  Using extended voice and microphone techniques along with electronics\, feedback\, field recordings\, and flutes\, they explore the outer dimensions of sound through performance. They create a present tense sound with a vigorous\, ritualistic delivery that seeks extreme release through physical exertion and psychic extension. They have collaborated with many other artists\, both in structured compositions and through their dedicated practice of improvisation. Their solo  releases include\nSapropelic Pycnic\n(Drag City 2017)\,\nRespires\n(RVNG Intl 2019)\,\nBrooding Exercises\n(Longform Editions 2021)\,\nVivification Exercises\n(RVNG Intl 2021)\, and\nBearings: Soundtracks for the Bardos\n(RVNG 2024). They have been a recipient of the Foundation of Contemporary Art’s Emergency Grant\,  a Jerome Foundation Artist-In-Residence at Roulette Intermedium\, and was a Jerome Hill Artist Fellow through 2023-25.\nLeila Bordreuil’s\nmusic accesses concepts from noise\, free jazz\, contemporary classical and other experimental traditions but adheres to no single genre. Mixing melancholic cello melodies with harsh noise walls\, she creates “steadily scathing music\, favoring long and corrosive atonalities.” (New York Times). Driven by a fierce interest in pure sound and inherent texture\, Leila challenges conventional cello practice through extreme extended techniques and amplification methods\, to the extent she sometimes seems to be playing the P.A rather than her cello. Her composed works frequently incorporate sound-spatialization by way of site-specific pieces and multichannel installations. Her panoramic vision of form and genre is reflected in the diversity of her collaborative projects\, which include artists such as Tamio Shiraishi (Fushitsuta)\, Lee Ranaldo (Sonic Youth)\, Kali Malone\, Laurel Halo\, Drew McDowall (Coil)\, Zach Rowden (Tongue Depressor)\, Micheal Foster\, Julia Santoli\, and Luke Stewart (Irreversible Entanglements).\nLuke Stewart\nis a musician\, performer\, improviser-composer\, organizer\, and writer-researcher whose work represents a deep reverence for the history and tradition of Creative Music: a tradition which encompasses the diverse styles of expression within the body of Black Music in the United States\, Africa\, and throughout the world. Stewart’s regular ensembles include Irreversible Entanglements\, SILT Trio\, Exposure Quintet\, and the experimental rock duo Blacks’ Myths\; he also performs regularly in numerous collaborations. Over the years\, Stewart has performed at Arts for Art’s Vision Festival\, New York\, NY\; Winter Jazzfest\, New York\, NY\; The Kennedy Center\, Washington\, D.C\; Rhizome DC\, Washington\, D.C.\; The Metropolitan Museum of Art\, New York\, NY\; Big Ears Festival\, Knoxville\, TN\; the BIMHUIS\, Amsterdam\, Netherlands\; and Roskilde Festival\, Roskilde\, Denmark\, among many other venues and festivals in the United States and abroad.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261218T010000Z
SUMMARY:Ka Baird & Chris Ryan Williams “Enthalpy” Record Release // Leila Bordreuil & Luke Stewart
UID:7f92450c-7a29-4657-90f1-915946a871b1@7f92.org
